  [
    "Friday, 7 November 1941",
    "Bill left for Manchester at noon, beginning a journey he had been looking forward to. The weather was good, but the trip itself proved tiring.\n\nFor five hours he had to stand on the train, making the long journey considerably less comfortable than he might have hoped. By the time he finally reached Manchester it was around midnight.\n\nBill turned in at about 1.30 a.m., tired after the travelling and ready for some sleep before making the most of his time there."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 8 November 1941",
    "After his long journey the previous day, Bill spent the morning walking around Manchester and properly seeing the sights.\n\nDuring his wanderings he stopped for a drink at two pubs, the Grove and the Cricketers Arms, before returning home at about 4pm. Later he went back to the Cricketers Arms and spent the evening there.\n\nIt turned into a lively night, with plenty of singing and drinking. Bill had what he described as a “swell time” and eventually turned in quite drunk, recording simply that it had been “a good night.”"
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 9 November 1941",
    "Bill did not get up until noon after the previous night at the Cricketers Arms. Once he had breakfast behind him, he called back there to say hello before heading into town.\n\nIt was a quiet and cold day. Bill had arranged several dates, but things did not go according to plan. In what he called “a mess-up,” he failed to keep any of them.\n\nHe did manage to obtain two films during the day. After the late start and the unsuccessful arrangements, Bill eventually turned in at about 1 a.m."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 10 November 1941",
    "Bill said farewell to the friends he had been staying with and went into central Manchester for his final day of leave. He went dancing at a place he recorded as “Rity,” which he thought was rather good and had “plenty of talent.” He reckoned Bert would have been in his element there.\n\nBill returned for tea at 5pm before catching the 7.10pm train back towards Plymouth. He had thoroughly enjoyed his stay and came away particularly impressed by the people of Manchester, whom he considered friendlier and more jovial than the people he had encountered in southern England.\n\nThe return journey involved changing trains twice, but compared with the exhausting trip north, when he had stood for five hours, Bill found the journey back “not so bad.”"
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 11 November 1941",
    "Bill's return from Manchester did not go entirely to plan. His train was late, leaving him two hours adrift when he finally returned to the naval establishment. After enjoying his leave in Manchester, he was sorry to be back.\n\nHe then discovered some particularly disappointing news: his group were the last of their batch of New Zealanders still left at Plymouth, while the others were already on their way home. Bill had been hoping for weeks that his own departure was getting closer.\n\nWhile Bill had been away, Bert's romantic life had apparently developed considerably. The boys told him that Bert “has it bad” and had “fallen hard”, suggesting Bert had become seriously taken with the girl he had been seeing.\n\nBill was on duty from noon until 4pm. His own thoughts were firmly on leaving England, writing “Blow the women, let me get on my way home.” A cake and mail from home were a welcome arrival that day.\n\nThe night was very misty and, for once, there were no air raids."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 12 November 1941",
    "The morning was misty and cold, although the sun managed to appear later in the day. Bill remained aboard and found that there was not a great deal happening.\n\nBert was still very taken with the floosie he had been seeing, and Bill described him as being “fair-dinkum” about her.\n\nThe weather became much wilder that night, with thunder and lightning. Four barrage balloons came down in flames before conditions finally eased at around midnight. Despite all the activity in the sky, there had been no air raid."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 13 November 1941",
    "The day was cloudy, with the G.O.'s report taking place. Bert was due to see his girlfriend at 1.30pm, so Bill did an hour of duty for him. Bill also lent money to some of the boys, including Bert, joking that his brother probably needed it to take his “floosie” out.\n\nBill went ashore at 6pm and met Joan. They went to the pictures to see Of Mice and Men, which Bill thought was “not bad.” Two air-raid warnings sounded while they were watching the film. He was back on board by 9.45pm.\n\nLater, Bill heard two accounts of the battles involving HMS Hood and Bismarck. He recorded being told that HMS Rodney had fired 350 16-inch shells at Bismarck, and that Prince of Wales had received a live shell in a boiler."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 14 November 1941",
    "Bill was up at 8am to fair weather and sunshine. After four weeks without it, the stove had finally been repaired, which was a welcome relief.\n\nHe was still waiting for a reply after writing to Manchester four weeks earlier. More importantly, he continued wondering when his draft home would arrive. By now it seemed certain that they were going, but there was still no word on when.\n\nBill appeared before the C.O. and was let off with a caution. He felt he had managed to “put one across over Pincher.”\n\nHe recorded that the Royal Oak had sunk while being towed. An air raid was on that night, with aircraft in the vicinity.\n\nBill also lent the cook 10 shillings, taking his watch as security for the loan."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 15 November 1941",
    "The fine weather continued. Bill went ashore at 6pm, met Joan and went with her to a dance. While there he met a poet, although Bill wasn't particularly impressed with the dance itself.\n\nThe evening with Joan did not go smoothly either, and the two had a row. Despite the argument, they arranged to meet again at 6pm on Monday.\n\nAn air raid took place that night, although nothing was dropped in Bill's area. He went on duty at midnight and did an extra two hours for Bert. During the watch he raided the galley for something to eat and finally turned in at 5.45am."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 16 November 1941",
    "It was raining heavily, and Bill hoped the weather would clear for the following day.\n\nBert's girlfriend had left for Bristol, but he was evidently determined to continue seeing her. He was already planning to travel there the following weekend for four days.\n\nOtherwise it was a very quiet day with little happening. Bill went on duty from 8pm until midnight. By then the weather had become particularly wild, with strong winds, thunder and lightning."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 17 November 1941",
    "The rain continued, although it wasn't as cold. Bill wasn't feeling particularly well that day.\n\nHe went for a stroll during the evening despite the misty conditions and returned on board at 10pm. At some point the lights at the establishment fused, leaving them in complete darkness until midnight."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 18 November 1941",
    "Bill swapped his watch duty with someone else so that he could go ashore that evening. He went dancing at the Embassy Dance and also visited the Britannia Hotel.\n\nDuring the evening he met a “swell blonde fairy” and also saw some of Bert's bus conductors. Bill had a particularly good time and decided that he “must visit there again.”\n\nJoe had also met a girl, so Bill left him behind to take his “floosie” home. Bill returned on board at 11.30pm."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 19 November 1941",
    "It was a pretty good day, although Bill was noticing how short the daylight hours had become. It was now after 8am before it became light and dark again by about 5.30pm. The weather remained cool.\n\nBill hoped to try for a substitute that night, which might allow him to go ashore.\n\nHe saw one of Bert's girlfriends and thought she was “not bad.” Later, Bill accompanied them to the bus before returning on board at midnight.\n\nThere was one air raid that night."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 20 November 1941",
    "The weather was reasonably good and not as cold. It was payday, with Bill receiving £2-12-0, or as he put it, “collected beans.”\n\nHe received the Weekly News from Billie and was expecting more mail to arrive soon.\n\nBill went ashore with Joe, Ben and Sconce, and they had a “swell time.” During the evening Bill met a girl named Penny and arranged a date with her.\n\nHe also managed to catch Ben out over the watches, something Bill clearly found amusing."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 21 November 1941",
    "Bill woke with “a bit of a thick head” after the previous night's outing. The weather was wild, with heavy rain and rough conditions, although it cleared around 11am and the sun came out.\n\nHe had been making plans to go ashore on Tuesday when, at 6.30pm, the long-awaited draft chit finally arrived. Bill didn't yet know where he was being sent and could only hope that it meant he was finally on his way home. Ben had received the news as well. Bert was away on leave and wouldn't be back until Tuesday, so Bill couldn't tell him until then. He hoped he could somehow get Bert drafted with him.\n\nThere was another surprise when Bill discovered that Jack Lynch was at the establishment looking for him.\n\nBill went on duty from 8pm until midnight. At first he thought the gunfire he could hear along the coast was gunnery practice, but quickly changed his mind, believing that a convoy was being shelled.\n\nThe raid then became very real. German aircraft were over the area, with heavy gunfire and shrapnel falling onto the roof of the mess decks. Bill admitted that he ducked as the fragments came down. The intense activity lasted for about fifteen minutes."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 22 November 1941",
    "It was raining again that morning, but Bill had received excellent news. His efforts to have Bert transferred with him had “bloomed.” Bert was being recalled from leave and was to report to the barracks at 10am on Monday, before joining ship with Bill on Tuesday for New Zealand. Bill could only hope that his brother would return in time.\n\nThe news that Bill and Bert were leaving was harder on the others. Ben took it badly, while Joe and Scouse were “in the dumps” about seeing them go.\n\nThat evening Bill, Ben, Scouse and Joe went ashore for a final spree together. An air raid interrupted proceedings, and Bill noted the unfortunate situation back at the establishment — there were no machine-gunners aboard because they were all out on the spree.\n\nBill met Penny, Jenny and Marie, and the group had a good time until a fight broke out involving some Australians. Ben was hit on the head with a bottle, Scouse suffered a cut lip, and Bill came away with a lump on his shin.\n\nAmid the confusion they managed to lose one another and then find each other again, eventually returning on board at midnight.\n\nBill then went to see the Officer of the Day, only to discover that Bert still hadn't been located and recalled from leave. With their departure approaching, Bill was exasperated, calling his brother “the fool” and wondering what would happen now."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 23 November 1941",
    "It was a fine day with the sun shining, but there was still no sign of Bert. Bill blamed “the woman” for his brother's absence and was becoming increasingly frustrated. He packed his kit ready to leave the following day and still hoped Bert would make it back in time to come with him, particularly after all the trouble Bill had gone to arranging his transfer.\n\nBill went for a stroll with Joan, but an air raid forced them to take cover in a hedge for protection from bombs and falling shrapnel. There was considerable anti-aircraft fire overhead.\n\nAfter seeing Joan home, Bill was caught in another raid on his way back. This time he witnessed an aerial fight between a Spitfire and a German bomber. Bill watched as the British fighter brought the bomber down in flames, followed by a large flash when it struck the ground. One German airman bailed out wounded, while the others were killed.\n\nThe air raid continued for about an hour. Bill noted that the German aircraft seemed to be coming over more frequently now. Despite the raid still being underway, he eventually turned in, knowing he had duty from midnight until 4am. By then everything was clear."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 24 November 1941",
    "With his kit packed, Bill caught the 9.45am boat to the barracks to report for his draft. It was finally time to leave Plymouth, and he said “cheerio” to the lads he had spent the previous months with, wishing them all the best.\n\nBert was still adrift and had not returned in time to leave with him, despite Bill's efforts over the previous few days to have his brother included in the same draft.\n\nAt 2pm, Bill boarded the train for the long journey north to Leith, Scotland."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 25 November 1941",
    "Bill arrived in Scotland at 12.30am after what he described as an awful train journey, with little opportunity for either food or sleep.\n\nLater he had dinner at the Leith Rest Home, which he thought was rather good. From there he proceeded to Leith and joined HMNZS Tui, taking his bags and hammock aboard the brand-new ship.\n\nWith Tui due to be commissioned the following day, Bill went ashore again. He had a good time, although he missed Bert and Ben, the two men who had been such a large part of his time at Plymouth.\n\nAfter a few drinks Bill was nearly drunk. He met a girl, accompanied her home, and rather than returning to Tui, slept ashore that night."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 26 November 1941",
    "Bill woke with “a bit of a thick head” after the previous night and had a reviver before breakfast. He was particularly taken with the “swell waitress” who served them.\n\nHe was aboard Tui by 8.45 a.m. At 9.30 a.m. the ship was commissioned, with prayers, a hymn and the National Anthem. From there the day became a busy one, with stores of every description and ammunition being brought aboard and engine trials carried out.\n\nBill had been told that they were expected to sail for New Zealand around the middle of January. Lieutenant Commander Hilliard was the captain, and Bill knew him from years earlier. He hoped Hilliard did not still hold anything against him or Bert, remembering that Bill had “acted the goat” when he had previously served under him.\n\nBill was on the port watch and had already formed a hunch that he might not like the ship. Even so, he thought it would be worth it if she got him home, after which he could put in for another draft. He drew his first issue aboard the new ship and finished the day tired after all the work."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 27 November 1941",
    "Work aboard Tui continued, with stores and equipment still being loaded and the ship gradually being prepared for service. Bill had now been given responsibility for the minesweeping gear.\n\nAt noon Bert finally arrived and joined the ship. Ben, however, remained back at Trevol. Hilliard gave Bert a lecture after his arrival, and Bert was then placed in the same watch as Bill.\n\nBill was on duty that night. The cold was bitter, and after the day’s work he turned in early when he had the opportunity."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 28 November 1941",
    "It was “cold as the devil” as preparations aboard Tui continued. Bill helped load ammunition and depth charges, and checked over the ship's minesweeping gear for which he had been given responsibility.\n\nFood aboard was rather meagre. There was enough to take away the hunger, but little more.\n\nBill and Bert went ashore that evening. Bert was broke, so Bill paid for their tea before they went dancing. Bill was beginning to discover that his brother was dear to take ashore. During the evening they met a couple of girls and arranged to see them again on Sunday afternoon.\n\nThe bitter cold continued, leaving Bill increasingly keen to get underway. They were back aboard at 11.30pm. With the brothers together again, Bill finished the day thinking of their other brother, wishing Harry was there with them."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 29 November 1941",
    "The cold was making getting out of his bunk increasingly difficult, and Bill was the last one up that morning, wishing he could have stayed there for another couple of hours.\n\nWork aboard Tui continued throughout the day, cleaning up the new ship and getting everything into its proper place. Bill worked fairly hard and also found time to write home.\n\nThere was no leave until 4pm, when all-night leave began, although Bill was out of luck as he was on duty through until the following day.\n\nThe food aboard was still insufficient, continuing his complaint from the previous day. Bill was already thinking about the decent meal he would have when he finally arrived home.\n\nHe noted that Ben was still writing to his “fairy.” Bill did some sewing before turning in early at 9pm."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 30 November 1941",
    "The morning began with church service aboard Tui. Bill obtained a Testament afterwards, which he kept as a souvenir of the occasion.\n\nHe went ashore at 1pm and met the two girls he and Bert had arranged to see on Friday. They spent the afternoon at the zoo, which Bill thought was not a bad place.\n\nThat evening they met another two girls and went to the Garrison Theatre, where they watched a concert that Bill also considered not bad.\n\nThe weather remained bitterly cold. Bill returned aboard Tui at midnight."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 1 December 1941",
    "Mail arrived, although everything Bill received was from England. Bert had a letter from Harry, which left Bill wondering whether some of his own mail had gone astray. Bert also received a letter from his girlfriend and was in particularly good spirits.\n\nBoth money and food were becoming scarce, and the cold remained severe. To make matters worse, there had been no hot water for more than a week.\n\nBill had now become the ship’s barber and was also growing a beard of his own. He and Mo were starting a dhobying firm as well, finding another way to keep themselves occupied and make a little money.\n\nThe day included gun drill and the making of head pieces for the minesweeping gear, adding to the continuing work of getting the ship ready."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 2 December 1941",
    "Work aboard Tui continued, with much of the day spent cleaning the ship. The weather remained bitterly cold, while the shortage of food was becoming a persistent complaint. Bill was almost always hungry, with much of what they were being served coming from tins.\n\nHe went ashore for a stroll and visited the pictures, getting some chips and a drink while he was out. It made for a pleasant evening away from the ship.\n\nBill was putting aside a few tots of alcohol to celebrate with Leon when he eventually arrived home. Otherwise he was broke until payday, apart from money he had deliberately reserved to buy something during the journey home.\n\nThere was still no mail from Plymouth. Bill had apparently expected to hear from someone there, but with nothing arriving he guessed that the girl had forgotten him, and decided that was probably for the best."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 3 December 1941",
    "The weather remained cold, and Bill noticed how low the sun stayed in the Scottish winter sky. Work aboard Tui continued, with Bill making more heaving lines and mixing paint as the crew prepared the new ship.\n\nSome welcome news arrived from home. Bill received a letter from New Zealand as well as a cablegram from Leon. He was pleased that Leon appeared to be taking his advice, although he gives no indication here what that advice concerned.\n\nBert remained very attached to his girlfriend. Bill thought their impending departure for New Zealand might actually be for the best. At the same time, efforts were apparently being made to get Ben transferred up from Trevol to join them.\n\nBill was nominated as Tui's ship's barber, a job he already knew well, having performed the same role on the ship that had brought him to Britain.\n\nConditions aboard Tui were still fairly primitive. There was still no steam and consequently no hot water, continuing a problem Bill had already been complaining about for more than a week.\n\nHe also heard the grim news that the Australian cruiser HMAS Sydney had been sunk with all hands."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 4 December 1941",
    "Work aboard Tui continued, with the crew still cleaning the ship and finding places for the stores and equipment that had been brought aboard. At 1.30pm, Tui moved to Albert Dock.\n\nBill went ashore at 4.30pm and had his hair cut by a woman. Afterwards he had half a pint and some chips before going to a social gathering. He had an enjoyable evening, met several girls and accompanied one of them home.\n\nBill returned aboard at 11.15pm, while Bert was still ashore with his girlfriend."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 5 December 1941",
    "Bill spent part of the day organising the minesweeping store, while still more equipment and supplies were brought aboard Tui. The weather remained cold but at least it was dry. He noticed two destroyers being built astern of them in the shipyard.\n\nConditions aboard were beginning to improve. Tui's engines were now operating, giving the ship hot water and her own electrical power at last. Bill was still growing his beard and also started the dhobying business he had planned with Mo a few days earlier. There was more gun practice, and the day remained busy as further stores arrived during the afternoon.\n\nBill was becoming increasingly unhappy with the ship's company and intended to apply for another draft once he reached New Zealand. He was particularly dissatisfied with the routine and the arrangement of the crew into two messes, and expected that arguments would eventually break out aboard.\n\nHe spent some time darning his socks before going ashore despite being on duty. After a couple of drinks, he went dancing still dressed in his overalls and had plenty of fun. Bill eventually returned aboard at 2am, while Bert was very drunk."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 6 December 1941",
    "The cold had become almost unbearable. Temperatures were around freezing, with a strong wind and intermittent rain, and Bill felt he had never experienced cold like it.\n\nBill and Bert went ashore together, had a few drinks and met a couple of girls. They spent an enjoyable evening together before returning aboard Tui at about 11pm for a short time, then heading ashore again to continue their night out.\n\nBill eventually returned aboard at 2am. He had intended to return with Bert, but after being kept waiting for 45 minutes he gave up on his brother and went back to the ship without him."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 7 December 1941",
    "There had been no afternoon leave the previous day, and Bill worked throughout Sunday. It was bitterly cold, and the ship was moved three times as preparations continued for going to sea, including taking on oil.\n\nBert managed to obtain a substitute for his watch so that he could go to the pictures. Things aboard were not going particularly well. There had not been enough food lately, and Bill felt that everything was going crook. He nevertheless managed to do some Christmas shopping. He also had some oysters that had been sent to Bert from Nestlé.\n\nBill was issued with seaboot socks and an ordinary [??] from comfort funds. Against these relatively routine events came much more significant news: he heard that Japan had declared war on America. His immediate concern was what the widening war might mean for their voyage, hoping that it would make no difference to their plans to return home."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 8 December 1941",
    "Tui put to sea at 8.50am, making her way out through the Leith docks. Bill was pleased with how the new ship manoeuvred during the day's trials.\n\nOnce at sea, the crew carried out depth-charge trials, which went successfully. The weather remained cold, and Tui returned to port at about 5pm.\n\nThe war news was troubling, making Bill and the others even more eager to begin their voyage home. That evening he went ashore to the local pictures.\n\nWhen Bill returned aboard, mail was waiting for him. There was correspondence from home and a parcel from the Patriotic Fund of Comforts, as well as a letter from South Africa and another from Madeline and Arthur."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 9 December 1941",
    "Bill was up at 6.45am and began the day scrubbing the decks while Tui was alongside in dock. The food continued to disappoint. Some of it was spoiled and the rice had not been properly cooked, although Bill managed to improve it himself. They were still living largely on canned food.\n\nBert spent the day cleaning the machine guns. He had been assigned as the gunner aft, giving him responsibility for the armament at the after end of the ship.\n\nTui moved to another dock during the day. Bill cut another sailor's hair and was now firmly established as the ship's barber, continuing a job he had also performed during his voyage to Britain.\n\nRumours were circulating that Tui might soon sail for New Zealand. Bill briefly slipped ashore for a drink and had also obtained a bottle of Leon's favourite drink, which he was keeping for him.\n\nDespite the machinery having provided hot water a few days earlier, the supply had failed again and there was now none at all."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 10 December 1941",
    "Tui left the jetty at 9am and proceeded to sea for further trials. Bill was issued another jersey and a pair of seaboots, welcome additions given the increasingly cold conditions. He spent part of the day cleaning and attending to the minesweeping gear.\n\nThe ship underwent compass testing, during which there was nearly a mishap, although Bill gives no details about what happened.\n\nThe day's war news was particularly depressing. Bill learned of the loss of the British battleship Prince of Wales and battlecruiser Repulse, as well as reports that four American warships had been lost. With the war now spreading rapidly through the Pacific, the news would have had particular significance for New Zealand sailors already anxious to get home.\n\nTui finished the day anchored at Granton. There was no leave that evening, and Bill had anchor watch while a strong wind was blowing. A Tom Bowler game was held aboard that night.\n\nBill also wrote home to Leon, his mother, and Harry."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 11 December 1941",
    "Tui weighed anchor at 8.30am and proceeded to sea for her gunnery trials. The trials went successfully, with five rounds fired, including one high-explosive round. The machine guns were also involved in the day's work.\n\nBert was responsible for the machine guns aft and had evidently impressed Bill with his ability, who considered him the best machine-gunner aboard Tui.\n\nConditions at sea were considerably rougher than during the previous day's trials. A strong wind was blowing and waves were regularly coming over Tui's bows. Bert was caught by one of them and ended up soaked through.\n\nBill had the middle watch that night. He received one letter from New Zealand and two from England before turning in at 8pm, hoping to get as much sleep as possible before being called for duty."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 12 December 1941",
    "Bill began the day on the middle watch, with a strong wind still blowing. After scrubbing down the decks, Tui weighed anchor and carried out further compass testing. The sea remained rough enough for waves to come over the bows, and Bill made some lifelines for additional safety on deck.\n\nDuring the passage Tui passed beneath the Forth Bridge, and Bill took a photograph, although the dull weather wasn't ideal for photography.\n\nThey eventually came alongside Scarba at Port Edgar. Bill was immediately unimpressed with the place, particularly after learning that they were expected to remain there for three weeks.\n\nHe went ashore to the canteen but didn't drink, instead continuing on to a concert, which proved quite enjoyable. On his way out he ran into Sue.\n\nThere is a possibility that all of them might yet make the voyage home together."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 13 December 1941",
    "Bill began work before daylight, scrubbing the decks and then rigging Tui's minesweeping gear. A good batch of mail arrived, and he spent a considerable amount of time replying to it. By the end of the day he had written 19 letters altogether.\n\nThere was an air raid during the day, although no damage was reported. Bert also had some welcome mail, receiving two letters.\n\nThe crew had now been told that they would remain at Port Edgar for three weeks. Life aboard was becoming a little more comfortable, with a radio now installed in the mess.\n\nBill was on duty that day. Some of the other sailors tried to persuade him to do the cooking, but he firmly declined. By evening the rain had returned and the wind was blowing strongly. He turned in at 10.30pm."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 14 December 1941",
    "With fine but cold weather, Bill took the opportunity to spend the day in Edinburgh. While there, he tried to make contact with Mrs Lee's sisters, although it appears he was unsuccessful.\n\nDuring the day he met three girls and arranged a date with them. After stopping for a drink, he met another three girls and went with them to a show, making for an enjoyable evening.\n\nThe restrictions at Port Edgar were proving frustrating. Unlike some of his earlier nights ashore, leave here ended at 10.30pm, which Bill felt brought his evenings with women to an abrupt end. He nevertheless arrived back aboard about half an hour late without getting into trouble."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 15 December 1941",
    "Bill spent part of the day rigging Tui's minesweeping gear and also attended a minesweeping instructional film. He was on duty and spent some time teaching another sailor how to splice rope.\n\nLater he went to the canteen for a couple of drinks before attending a pantomime at Port Edgar. It proved to be a particularly enjoyable show, with Bill especially appreciating the female performers. He returned aboard Tui at 9.30pm.\n\nThere was some trouble involving another member of the crew, who had ended up in cells. Bill was distinctly suspicious of the coxswain and considered him underhanded, although he doesn't explain what had happened between the coxswain and the detained sailor.\n\nBill read for a while before turning in at 10.30pm."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 16 December 1941",
    "Another ship came alongside Tui during the morning but was not properly under control, nearly striking both the jetty and another vessel in the process.\n\nBill asked the Second Lieutenant for a set of hair clippers, further establishing his role as Tui's barber. He came off duty at 9am. The sun appeared later in the day, but conditions remained bitterly cold.\n\nMuch of Bill's day was devoted to training. He spent four hours on the machine gun and took part in minesweeping practice, with the gear working satisfactorily.\n\nDuring the day he met Lieutenant Palmer, skipper of Cromarty, one of the fleet sweepers, and had a conversation with him.\n\nThere was another incident while coming in, when Tui burst a pudding on the gripe. Bill gives no indication that it caused any serious consequences.\n\nBy evening he had a slight headache and was considering an early night, although the possibility of another concert was enough to leave him undecided."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 17 December 1941",
    "Tui left the jetty at 9am and proceeded to sea for minesweeping trials. As part of the exercise, the crew laid Dan buoys, marking positions in the water during the sweep. Conditions were considerably better than they had been recently, with a mild and pleasant day.\n\nBill also learnt a new type of rope splice, which he records as the Liverpool Salvage splice.\n\nTui returned at 2.30pm but was alongside for only a short time. At 4pm she put out again and proceeded towards the Forth Bridge for mine watching, with the crew expecting to remain out that night.\n\nThere was no mail for Bill that day. By evening the conditions were calm but slightly misty. He had the middle watch from midnight until 4am, serving as the port lookout at the machine-gun position."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 18 December 1941",
    "Bill's middle watch was cold and misty, with a cup of cocoa providing some warmth during the night.\n\nLater he checked over Tui's minesweeping gear in preparation for another trip to sea. The mist was too thick for minesweeping, however, and the day's exercise was postponed. Instead, Bill occupied himself with various jobs in the minesweeping store.\n\nHis dhobying business also began operating that day, earning him five shillings from washing clothes for other members of the ship's company."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 19 December 1941",
    "Bill found the jetty covered in white frost, the first he had seen since arriving in Scotland. A substantial quantity of supplies was loaded aboard Tui, including 30 casks of rum, cigarettes and Tickler tobacco.\n\nIt was also payday. Tui spent another day at sea carrying out minesweeping trials in excellent conditions, with sunshine and a calm sea.\n\nThere was some tension among the crew. Bert nearly became involved in a fight, while Bill's opinion of the coxswain had deteriorated further.\n\nBill was on duty that night and continued his small laundry business, this time doing dhobying for the officers. He was also beginning to think seriously about “going for the hook”, suggesting he was considering advancement to a higher rate.\n\nBert, meanwhile, remained preoccupied with the women he had been seeing."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 20 December 1941",
    "Bill went ashore at 1pm and travelled into Edinburgh, where he spent some time looking around Woolworths. After stopping for a few drinks, he met a New Zealand man and his wife. When Bill's companions arrived, the group spent the next couple of hours having a lively time together.\n\nHe later returned towards Queensferry and went to a dance at the Town Hall. On the bus he and a companion met two girls, joined up with them and took them for a drink.\n\nBert arrived later accompanied by two A.T.S. women. Bill was still somewhat aggrieved with his brother, who had apparently interfered with his chances with a girl earlier in the evening. Bill eventually accompanied another girl home before returning aboard Tui at 12.30am, exhausted. Bert had already made it back to the ship.\n\nThere was mail waiting from Irene: a Christmas card, photograph and letter for Bill, as well as a card from her for Bert.\n\nMeanwhile, Tui's football team had lost its match, although the sailors apparently compensated for the defeat by making off with a turkey."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 21 December 1941",
    "Bill was feeling the effects of the previous night's outing, waking with a thick head and generally below par. There was some light rain during the day.\n\nThe turkey acquired by the crew after the previous day's football match now required preparing, and the others turned to Bill because they apparently didn't know how to clean it themselves.\n\nBill continued his dhobying work and recorded that his earnings had now reached 32 shillings, a considerable increase from the five shillings he had made by 18 December.\n\nFeeling tired, he decided on an earlier night. The evening aboard was otherwise quiet, with the men sitting around spinning yarns before Bill turned in."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 22 December 1941",
    "There was little happening aboard Tui during the day. Bill spent the morning watching an instructional film on gunnery and felt that he had learnt something useful from it.\n\nHe went ashore at 5.30pm and bought a couple of Christmas cards, including one for Irene, returning the gesture after receiving her Christmas card, photograph and letter two days earlier.\n\nBill then went to a dance, where he had a few drinks and enjoyed himself reasonably well. He met an A.T.S. woman who was an excellent dancer and spent some time with her.\n\nThe evening ran beyond his permitted leave again. Bill eventually returned aboard Tui 45 minutes late."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 23 December 1941",
    "Tui put to sea at 9.30am for one-inch calibre aiming practice. Conditions were rough, and during the exercise Bill had to transfer from Tui to Inchkeith in a small sea boat. The little boat was thrown about considerably by the waves, making the transfer an uncomfortable experience, although Bill felt he had managed reasonably well.\n\nHe returned to Tui at about 5.30pm, with the sea still rough. On the way to their overnight anchorage, the crew carried out a night firing exercise with the 4-inch gun, which again went well.\n\nTui anchored off Dunbar for the night. Bill had anchor watch at 3am, by which time the weather had deteriorated into a severe gale. The wind was so strong that Bill was actually blown over the winch at one point."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 24 December 1941",
    "The weather remained extremely rough as Tui continued her exercises at sea. The day's programme included both anti-aircraft firing and another exercise conducted in the strong winds. Bill considered the anti-aircraft shoot satisfactory, although the second exercise produced only fair results.\n\nTui then had to take over towing the target used during the exercises. Recovering it in the rough conditions proved particularly difficult. It took the crew nearly an hour to pick it up, and in the process they lost a grappling hook and several lines.\n\nWith the exercises finally completed, Tui headed back towards port as quickly as possible, everyone hoping to get in before dark and have both Christmas Eve and Christmas Day ashore. Bill was not particularly confident that this would happen and wondered how much leave they would actually receive. There was still work waiting for them, with a large quantity of stores needing to be brought aboard.\n\nBill was relieved that the shooting exercises were finally over. The previous three days at sea had been exhausting for the crew, with rough weather, gunnery practice, target work and watches leaving them tired and worn out.\n\nAdding to the discomfort was the food. Bill was thoroughly dissatisfied with what they had been receiving while at sea: there was not enough of it, it was badly cooked, and much of what they were served was tinned food."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 25 December 1941",
    "Christmas Day had finally arrived, and Bill found himself celebrating it in Scotland. In one sense it fulfilled an ambition of his: he had wanted to experience a Christmas in the British Isles. Yet now that the day had come, the novelty meant little compared with being away from home. Given the choice, there was nowhere he would rather have been than back in New Zealand. He wished everyone at home a Merry Christmas and hoped their day was better than his.\n\nThere were at least some efforts to make Christmas aboard Tui special. A fellow New Zealander had arranged a party that evening for all the New Zealanders aboard the ship. The officers provided a bottle of beer apiece, and the crew had their turkey for Christmas dinner. The skipper also conducted prayers before carrying out his rounds.\n\nChristmas mail brought some welcome news. Ben had written to say that he had received a draft to a destroyer, meaning he would not be joining Bill and Bert aboard Tui as Bill had earlier hoped. A letter also arrived from Billie in New Zealand.\n\nThere was still no word from Harry. Bill had been writing to him frequently but had not heard from him for a long time. On Christmas Day, with home particularly prominent in his thoughts, he could only hope that Harry was all right."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 26 December 1941",
    "Boxing Day brought a welcome change in the weather. The sun was shining and it was a lovely day, although still bitterly cold, with thick frost covering the jetty.\n\nThere was little opportunity for another day of Christmas celebrations aboard Tui. The crew spent time cleaning the ship in preparation for rounds by the Captain of the Base the following day.\n\nBill managed to get ashore that evening, but only for about half an hour. After returning aboard he settled back into some of the small jobs that had become part of his routine, doing more dhobying and haircutting.\n\nThe remainder of the evening was quiet. Bill wrote a couple of letters before turning in at about 11 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 27 December 1941",
    "The Captain of the Base carried out his rounds that morning and found very little to criticise. Bill recorded the verdict as “too tiddly — too good for us.”\n\nAt 11.45 a.m. Tui left Port Edgar and returned to Leith, arriving at about 1.15 p.m. The cold was severe enough for Bill to notice ice on the water.\n\nBert was supposed to be on duty but managed to sneak ashore. Bill remained aboard and settled into a long session of dhobying and ironing, continuing until about 11.30 that night.\n\nThe money from the work was beginning to mount up. Bill also fitted in the other ordinary jobs around the ship before finally finishing for the night."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 28 December 1941",
    "Bill went ashore at 1 p.m. with Bert and paid a visit to Mrs Leo’s sister. The winter weather remained bitterly cold, with ice still visible on both the water and the jetty. They had a couple of drinks during the visit and spent what Bill considered a very pleasant afternoon with Mrs Leo’s sister and her son, describing them as great people.\n\nAt about 6.30 p.m. Bill and Bert left to meet some of the other boys and girls. Bill already had a date arranged for the evening, and the group went to a concert together. Afterwards they had a simple supper of coffee and chips.\n\nBill left the girls at about 11 p.m., having arranged another date for a future evening. He was back aboard Tui by 11.45 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 29 December 1941",
    "The cold at Leith was severe, with almost everything frozen, although the sun appeared for a while and otherwise made it a lovely day. Conditions aboard Tui were miserable. There was no water aboard and they had been without steam for seven days, leaving Bill feeling the cold keenly.\n\nIt was a make-and-mend day, but Bill was on duty. He tried to get a bath at a sailors’ home ashore, only to find that he was too late.\n\nBert managed to sneak ashore to meet a girl. Bill was already looking ahead to New Year’s Eve, hoping they would be allowed ashore as he had a swell date arranged.\n\nLater, the men sat together talking and looking at photographs from home. The others produced photographs of their wives and girlfriends, but Bill was the only one without one to show. Despite his recent dates and acquaintances ashore, he considered himself to have no girlfriend. He spent some of the remaining time writing letters."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 30 December 1941",
    "There was still no steam or hot water aboard Tui, and Bill again remarked on just how cold it was. He went ashore at 5 p.m. and made another attempt to get a bath. This time he inadvertently walked into the ladies’ baths, an error he recorded with an embarrassed “ahem.” In the end he still didn't manage to have a bath and instead finished up in a pub.\n\nLater he went to the Eldorado Dance. He had a fair time, with a few drinks and several rums, and thought there were some attractive women there. The evening nearly ended in a fight when Bert became somewhat cantankerous, but the situation settled down without anything serious happening.\n\nBill was back aboard at midnight."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 31 December 1941",
    "Bill's hopes for New Year's Eve ashore came to nothing when he found himself on duty. With only two able seamen aboard, there was little prospect of getting away for the evening. He lent Bert 25 shillings and another man £1.\n\nHe intended to walk ashore later for a couple of drinks, but New Year itself was spent aboard Tui. At midnight they rang sixteen bells to mark the occasion. Bill consoled himself with the thought that he would have to make up for it with a good time the following day.\n\nThe men remaining aboard raided the ship's safe for something decent to eat for supper. Bill's thoughts again turned homeward, hoping everyone there was having a swell time.\n\nMeanwhile, the first of the men who had enjoyed New Year's Eve ashore began returning to the ship—absolutely drunk."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 1 January 1942",
    "The men wished one another a Happy New Year, and Bill finally got ashore at 1 p.m. After his unsuccessful attempts to find a bath over the previous two days, he managed to have one at the Victoria League and thought it was really good.\n\nFrom there he went to the Palais de Danse, where there was a swell crowd and, in Bill's estimation, some very attractive women. He went to tea with a couple of girls, then obtained a few drinks at the Naval Club. He was also carrying some rum of his own.\n\nLater he called on another couple of girls, whom he found still in bed recovering from the previous night's New Year celebrations. Bill returned to the Palais de Danse and had a fair time there before eventually leaving with three girls.\n\nHaving missed the last tram, he faced a four-mile walk back to Tui and finally arrived aboard at 2 a.m. Bert was already in his bunk and apparently had not had a particularly successful night. Bill also remembered to send a cablegram home for the New Year."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 2 January 1942",
    "Bill was a little tired that morning after spending about six hours dancing the previous day. Tui put to sea at 11 a.m. and later tied up at Granton, although they were due to leave again that night, much to Bill's annoyance.\n\nHe spent some of the day splicing and was becoming confident in his ability, remarking that he didn't think anyone else aboard could splice particularly well.\n\nMore importantly, Bill knew that a demanding period was approaching. He expected fourteen days of hard work on a special escort, suggesting that Tui was about to undertake a significant assignment away from her recent trials and local work.\n\nHe also found time for some more dhobying before turning in at 11.30 p.m. It was a clear night with brilliant moonlight."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 3 January 1942",
    "Tui was now anchored in Convoy Bay, where Bill was standing anchor watches. A fair number of ships had gathered there and, as he understood it, they were making up the escort. For Bill, this marked the point where the job began in earnest.\n\nThere was also a new first lieutenant aboard. Bill was not impressed by his enthusiasm for work, describing him as a fair cow and apparently finding him rather inclined to be overbearing.\n\nConditions were beginning to get a little rough, with white horses appearing on the water. Despite the increased work, Bill's dhobying business continued to pay, bringing him another pound.\n\nMail from home finally reached him, although it had been posted back in October. Bill also recorded the loss of Neptune."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 4 January 1942",
    "Rain and rough weather greeted Bill as the voyage got underway. Lifelines were rigged around Tui as an additional precaution in the worsening conditions. The ship was operating with only two watches, meaning a demanding routine of four hours on duty followed by four hours off.\n\nBill hoped they would have a decent passage around the north of Scotland to Tokomaru. At 4 p.m. the pipe was given to stand down, and with plenty of work expected later, Bill intended to get some sleep while he could.\n\nConditions subsequently became extremely rough. Bill thought the North Sea lived up to—and exceeded—its reputation. Bert was the first aboard to become seasick, although he recovered afterwards.\n\nBill then injured himself . The injury was serious enough for him to spend the remainder of the passage in bed, with orders to report to hospital when they reached their destination.\n\nRather than write individual entries for the following days, Bill later noted that from then until 12 January every day was practically the same. From sick bay, however, he remembered experiencing some excellent weather during part of the passage."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 12 January 1942",
    "Bill was discharged from hospital at 1 p.m. and was back aboard Tui by 2.15. Looking around Tobermory Bay, he noted the surrounding hills topped with snow and believed that Tobermory itself was an island.\n\nWhile Bill had been in hospital and sick bay, the rest of Tui's company had been having a gruelling time. The intensive work was still continuing when he returned, although Bill was excused from the worst of it and placed on light duties while he recovered from his injury."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 13 January 1942",
    "Action stations were called that morning, although Bill, still on light duties following his injury and hospital stay, did not take part. The afternoon was considerably easier, with make-and-mend giving him time for his own affairs.\n\nHe used much of it for an extraordinary amount of correspondence. Bill finished the second episode of his letter home, which had reached 81 pages, and also sent Leon what he called a short note—despite it running to 12 pages. He wrote another one or two letters home before finally turning in at 11 p.m.\n\nA welcome parcel also arrived from New Zealand: a tin of cigarettes and tobacco sent by the New Zealand Patriotic Fund."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 14 January 1942",
    "Bill was up at 6 a.m. and Tui put to sea at 8.50. There was a fair swell running and the ship was rolling, although the weather itself was good and the sun was shining.\n\nThe day's exercises included a practice submarine attack on Tui and an M.L. Bill was clearly feeling the pace of the training, noting that they still had another seven days to go. He had little affection for the ship's “Jimmy,” whom he considered a hard taskmaster. By the end of the day's work everyone was tired.\n\nBill had the 2–5 a.m. watch, so he turned in to get some sleep before going on duty."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 15 January 1942",
    "Tui returned to sea for another full day of training, with further manoeuvres and another practice submarine attack. Conditions were considerably less pleasant than the previous day. It was bitterly cold and a gale was blowing.\n\nAfter returning from the day's exercises, Bill wrote home before turning in at 10.30 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 16 January 1942",
    "The gale was still blowing and visibility remained poor. Despite the conditions, the morning was taken up with exercises, followed by lectures during the afternoon. There was even a little snow during the day.\n\nBill received a letter from M.B.M. containing the “Home News.” Later, he had to pull the liberty men ashore, taking those going on leave from the ship to land. Back aboard, he cut the skipper's hair, something he recorded with an amused “ha! ha!”, and did some more dhobying.\n\nBy later in the day, the weather had calmed."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 17 January 1942",
    "Bill began the day listening to the German news on the radio, including a broadcast by Lord Haw-Haw. Later, Tui was back at sea for more training and carried out a shoot, which Bill thought went particularly well.\n\nIt remained bitterly cold, and the hills around Tobermory were now covered with snow. Bill was looking forward to leaving, hoping that they would soon begin the long trip home to New Zealand. He expected they would get some leave when they reached Greenock.\n\nHis sideline doing the crew's dhobying was continuing to pay off. After doing some more washing that day, Bill recorded that the little enterprise had now accumulated £5 between the two of them."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 18 January 1942",
    "It was a marvellous day at Tobermory, with the sun shining, but there was little respite from the training. Tui carried out still more exercises, and the boys were becoming thoroughly browned off with the relentless routine.\n\nHe cleaned his gun in preparation for another shoot. The combination of hard work and increasingly meagre food was beginning to wear on him, and he noted that there had not been much to eat lately despite the amount of work they were doing.\n\nThere was little time for anything else. Bill had to go on watch, putting off his letter home until the following day."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 19 January 1942",
    "Bill was relieved to know that Tui would finally be leaving Tobermory sometime that week. He spent part of the day cleaning out the minesweeping store and attended lectures in first aid and other subjects.\n\nThe weather was miserable, with rain and a strong wind, but Tui was nevertheless due to put to sea for a full-calibre shoot. Conditions deteriorated further and snow began falling, forcing the shoot to be cancelled.\n\nBy now Bill had had enough of Tobermory. Cold, overworked and thoroughly fed up with the place, he could only hope that the weather would improve the following day."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 20 January 1942",
    "Tui proceeded to sea at 7.30 a.m. for another day of training. The weather was reasonably good, although there was still a fair swell running. Once again the day's exercises included a practice submarine attack.\n\nThe relentless programme was taking its toll. Bill was just about exhausted and bitterly cold, with duty still ahead of him that night. Bert was also feeling unwell, and the men believed that the poor food they were being given was responsible."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 21 January 1942",
    "Tui weighed anchor at 8.30 a.m. and headed out to sea in appalling conditions. A fierce wind was blowing and the seas were extremely rough, breaking over the bow, stern and gunwales and leaving Bill soaked through. To make matters worse, it was bitterly cold and snowing.\n\nThe conditions also made handling the ships hazardous. Tui narrowly avoided a collision, escaping with little more than a scrape, while a tanker was not so fortunate and struck another ship. The weather was too rough for Tui to remain secured in port, forcing her back out to sea.\n\nEmergency watches were maintained that night, and Bill turned in fully dressed so that he could respond immediately if required. His thoughts went home to New Zealand, where he hoped everyone was enjoying better weather. By now he had had enough of Tobermory: there had been no leave, the weather was awful, and the food was poor. Nearly everyone was complaining of an upset stomach."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 22 January 1942",
    "Snow lay all around Tobermory and it remained bitterly cold. Bill received some welcome mail from home before Tui carried out the full-calibre shoot that the weather had prevented three days earlier.\n\nThis time the shooting went exceptionally well. Bill proudly recorded that Tui achieved the best shoot at Tobermory Bay. There was more work to come, however, with another exercise at 5 p.m. followed by blackout drills that night.\n\nThe news Bill had been waiting for finally came: Tui was due to leave for Greenock the following day. Before turning in, he wrote home."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 23 January 1942",
    "At last, Tui's time at Tobermory was coming to an end. The final day began with a Commodore's inspection, requiring the ship to be thoroughly cleaned before the crew were put through one last run-through.\n\nAt 5 p.m. Tui sailed for Greenock, which Bill regarded as the first stage of their journey home. Once underway they were out in the Atlantic, expecting to reach Greenock at about 6 a.m. the following morning. It was still bitterly cold, with light rain and snow covering the hills as they left Tobermory behind. Bill went on duty again at 8 p.m.\n\nThe mood aboard was one of relief. Everyone was thankful to have finally left Tobermory and was now impatient for the sailing orders that would send them properly on their way home to New Zealand. The food aboard remained poor, making the parcels they received from home a godsend and especially welcome."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 24 January 1942",
    "Tui arrived at Greenock at 10.30 a.m. after what Bill considered a reasonably good trip. The ship was oiled while still out in the stream before proceeding alongside the jetty.\n\nBill finally got ashore at 5 p.m., but Greenock offered a rather bleak welcome. There was snow and slush everywhere, deep enough to come over their ankles as they made their way through the town. He had a few drinks, but was distinctly unimpressed with the place.\n\nBy 10 p.m. Bill had had enough and was back aboard."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 25 January 1942",
    "Bill went ashore at 3 p.m., with snow still lying on the ground around Greenock. After the poor food aboard Tui, he appreciated getting a decent meal ashore before going to a concert.\n\nThe evening ran later than planned and Bill missed the last bus, leaving him to get a taxi back. There seemed to be no shortage of women around Greenock, prompting him to think of his brothers Leon and Harry and wish that they were there to enjoy the night with him."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 26 January 1942",
    "Snow fell again as the cold weather continued at Greenock. Bill spent part of the day painting Tui and had duty that night, but his thoughts were firmly on the following morning.\n\nAfter the demanding weeks at Tobermory, Bill was finally getting a substantial break: nine days' leave. He planned to travel south to London and was considering calling at Manchester along the way. With the trip ahead of him, he turned in for the night."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 27 January 1942",
    "Bill's nine days of leave began with some unexpected news. Lieutenant-Commander Hilliard came aboard Tui, and the crew learned that the ship would now be remaining in Scotland for another month. They were waiting for other ships to arrive and, in the meantime, would be doing convoy work. Bill's hoped-for journey home was therefore delayed once again.\n\nBill left Tui at 1 p.m. and arrived in Glasgow at 2.30, where he stayed overnight. That evening he went to the Locarno Dance Hall and had a splendid time. He noted, apparently for Leon's benefit, that he had successfully “clicked” with a girl. He returned to his digs at 2 a.m., finishing the entry with an enthusiastic “Wacko!”"
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 28 January 1942",
    "Bill spent a little more time looking around before leaving Glasgow. At 2 p.m. he travelled via Edinburgh for Newcastle, arriving there at 7.15 that evening.\n\nAfter getting something to eat, he went dancing at the Oxford Galleries. His beard attracted a small amount of attention, but it certainly didn't interfere with his evening. Bill had another splendid night and was particularly impressed by some of the women there, joking to Leon that there were some good “magazine covers” among them.\n\nAt the end of the evening he saw a couple of the women home."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 29 January 1942",
    "Despite being on leave, Bill found himself awake by 7.30 a.m. He grumbled that he couldn't stay in bed any longer than he could aboard ship.\n\nHe spent part of the day exploring Newcastle and tried to have a look around the Vickers-Armstrongs munitions works, but didn't have sufficient identification papers to be admitted. Even from what he could see, he was impressed by the size of the place.\n\nThe afternoon proved much more successful. Bill fell in with an Australian, a Canadian and two Polish servicemen and had a splendid afternoon in their company. He also managed a look around Newcastle Brewery, an experience that earned an enthusiastic “Wacko!”\n\nThat evening he went dancing again. He finished the day's entry on a more personal note, remembering his mother's birthday: “Many happy returns Mum.”"
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 30 January 1942",
    "Bill spent another fairly quiet day wandering around Newcastle. He was feeling tired, and the weather did little to improve matters, with heavy snow and bitter cold continuing.\n\nDuring the day he visited the Crow's Nest pub. Bert had apparently met a reasonably promising girl, whom Bill described in his usual understated fashion as a “fair effort.”\n\nBill was also still trying to persuade Bert to come with him to Manchester. Manchester had only been a possible stop when Bill began his leave on the 27th, but he was clearly becoming more determined to make the visit."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 31 January 1942",
    "Bill and Bert left for London, enduring a bitterly cold journey south. Bill's feet were freezing, yet he lent his coat to a WAAF and a NAAFI girl who were also suffering badly from the cold.\n\nThey arrived in London at 7.30 p.m., but finding somewhere to sleep proved difficult. Unable to obtain a bed, Bill left his baggage at the YMCA, which he felt was doing its best to help them.\n\nHe then went dancing at the Paramount Dance Hall, where he met a girl and later saw her home. He considered her “not bad.”\n\nWhen Bill returned, the YMCA still had no bed available, so he spent the night sleeping on a chair. Bert had even less conventional accommodation and slept in a Tube station. Despite the freezing journey and their problems finding beds, London itself was mild that night with a bright moon."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 1 February 1942",
    "Bill was up at 5.45 a.m. to find London covered in heavy snow, about four inches deep. After the previous night’s unsuccessful search for accommodation, he returned to the U.J.C. and managed to secure a room for the night.\n\nHe spent part of the day looking around London and bought a couple of souvenirs. Dinner and tea were both taken at the New Zealand Forces Club.\n\nDuring the day Bill met a couple of Wrens and later ran into Jack Lynch. He then teamed up with two WAAFs, one of whom was Welsh, recording simply that he “did alright too.”\n\nWith his time in London coming to an end, Bill was preparing to leave for Manchester the following day."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 2 February 1942",
    "Bill arrived in Manchester at 5.20 p.m. and went to the YMCA, where he was evidently able to find accommodation.\n\nThat evening he headed out dancing at the “Pity.” After a couple of drinks he was feeling pretty good and spent the evening meeting a number of women, although he didn't take any of them home. Bert apparently had more success and did.\n\nBill also mentions meeting a couple of “Hotters”. After the evening out, he was in bed by midnight."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 3 February 1942",
    "Bill decided to spend the day visiting relations while Bert stayed in town. He first called on Gerti, who gave him dinner and a tablecloth to take home to his mother in New Zealand. He then called on Edwin, but found him out and was unable to obtain Bill’s address. From there he visited the Taylors, who were pleased to see him, and met their son.\n\nBill next went to Harry’s place, where only young Des was home, so he continued on to Harry’s workplace. After having a look around he found Harry, who was greatly surprised to see him. Harry showed Bill more of the factory and introduced him to the managers and some of the “big noises”. Bill watched the workforce and the production procedure, remarking that there was “some fair talent” working there. The factory was Crossley Motors, and he was particularly interested in seeing how the Avro Anson bomber was made from the raw materials.\n\nBill met Harry again after work and went home with him for tea, where he met Harry’s mother, whom he described as a “gay sort”. Still wondering where Bert had got to, Bill and Harry went to the “Pity” before returning home, where Bill stayed the night. He also encountered Edwin again, describing him as a “hard-case”.\n\nBill later said goodbye and went to meet Bert at the station, but their train was running late, so they returned to Harry’s. Bert was pleased to meet him, while Glady was surprised to see them return as she was in the middle of washing. Bill gave her their day’s rations for allowing them to stay overnight.\n\nHarry, Bert and Bill then went to Belle Vue, but found “nothing doing there”. They called on Irene, who was pleased to see them, before returning home for tea and taking a stroll prior to catching the train. Harry’s father arrived home shortly before they left. Young Harry had taken the day off work to spend it with them. Bill said his farewells, wondering whether they might one day meet again. Young Harry, he noted, was keen on coming to New Zealand."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 4 February 1942",
    "After his brief leave visit to Manchester, Bill began the return journey north. He and Bert left Manchester at 1.10 a.m., travelling through the night and arriving in Glasgow at 10.00 a.m. after a journey of nearly nine hours."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 5 February 1942",
    "Bill spent some time strolling around Glasgow before going to the Paramount Theatre. There he had some fun with the usherettes and managed to make a couple of dates. He returned aboard at 7.30 p.m., browned off at having the Middle Watch that night."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 6 February 1942",
    "There was nothing much happening during the day. The second watch went on leave, while Bill remained aboard carrying out G.M. duties. Later he went ashore and spent the evening dancing, where he met a couple of “fairies”. He accompanied them to their bus afterwards and arranged a date for Sunday before returning aboard at midnight."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 7 February 1942",
    "Bill was up at 7.30 a.m. and cleaned the mess before settling into the usual routine aboard. He answered his mail and was pleased to receive a tin of biscuits from his mother in New Zealand. Bert also received a parcel from her.\n\nBill stayed aboard for the remainder of the day and spent some of his time writing home. He turned in at 10.30 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 8 February 1942",
    "There was now both a dog and a cat aboard. The weather was fair and the sun was shining, although snow still covered the ground and surrounding hills. Bill had a date and went to a concert, which he thought was not bad. Afterwards he was unable to get a taxi and had to walk three miles back to the ship. He then went straight on duty, remaining on watch until 4.00 a.m."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 9 February 1942",
    "Bill spent part of the day painting the ship grey, with a little rain falling. There was some excitement when the dog, only mentioned for the first time the previous day, fell overboard.\n\nThat evening Bill went on a blind date and had a pretty good time. He stayed out too late and missed the last train, leaving him without his normal means of getting back to the ship. He eventually managed to get as far as Gourock aboard a Daily Express newspaper van. He finally made it back aboard at 5.40 a.m., summing up the adventure simply: “What a night.”"
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 10 February 1942",
    "Bill spent another day painting the ship and following the usual routine aboard. With only fourteen men aboard at present, the food had been good lately and, for once, he had no complaints on that score.\n\nThere was a little snow and some hail during the day, although neither amounted to very much. The weather seemed to be improving, and Bill wondered whether that was really the case or whether he was simply becoming more accustomed to the cold.\n\nHe turned in at 7 p.m. to get some sleep before going on duty at midnight. That watch would be longer than usual, as Bill was also doing a couple of extra hours as a substitute for someone else."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 11 February 1942",
    "Work aboard Tui continued much as it had over the previous few days, with Bill and the others still occupied painting the ship. Five French corvettes were now lying alongside them, while the weather remained reasonably good.\n\nBy evening Bill was ready for another night ashore. He reached Glasgow at about 6.40 p.m., had a few wets and soon met a couple of fairies. After having supper together, the evening developed into quite a night and proved expensive in the process. Despite the cost, Bill had a good time.\n\nHe eventually returned aboard at about half past midnight, having made quite a number of dates during the course of the evening."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 12 February 1942",
    "After the previous night in Glasgow, Bill woke with what he described as a rather thick head. The morning passed with the usual routine aboard, and by the afternoon he was ready to put his head down and get some sleep.\n\nPayday came at a useful time. Bill was not actually broke, although the previous evening had forced him to “crack the bank” and dip into the money he had been keeping in reserve.\n\nBert received a parcel, while Bill recorded that he received “the tiki.” During the day there was also talk that three German submarines had been destroyed while attempting to enter the Clyde.\n\nAfter the late night and the morning’s routine, Bill was content to get some rest."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 13 February 1942",
    "Painting aboard Tui continued for another day, with the weather remaining reasonably good. Once their work was finished, Bill and Bert went ashore together, travelling first to Paisley and then on to Glasgow.\n\nDuring the evening they visited a fun fair, but the outing proved rather disappointing and the night was not particularly enjoyable. They returned aboard at about 12.20 a.m."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 14 February 1942",
    "Bill was on duty today, although he managed to arrange for someone to substitute for him for an hour, giving him an opportunity to get ashore between watches.\n\nThere was also a mishap involving a revolver when one of the men pulled the trigger.\n\nWhile ashore Bill visited the Queen’s Hotel and later tried to get into a dance. By 8.45 p.m. there were crowds outside every dance hall, making his chances of getting in fairly slim. He returned aboard at 9.50 p.m., with another spell of duty waiting for him at four o’clock the following morning."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 15 February 1942",
    "After the morning's duties, the order to pipe down came at 11.30 a.m., leaving Bill free to make another attempt at getting ashore. He met Doris and went to see Million Dollar Baby. They spent an enjoyable afternoon and evening together.\n\nHis plans to return to the ship that night came unstuck when he discovered that the last train had already gone at 9.50 p.m., rather than at 11 p.m. as he had expected. With no way of getting back aboard, Bill spent the night ashore at the Allied Seamen’s Rest.\n\nHe was up early the following morning and back aboard by seven o’clock."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 16 February 1942",
    "The weather was fairly good as the work of painting and cleaning Tui continued. Bert was recovering from a hectic night out, while Bill was soon looking forward to another evening ashore himself.\n\nBill met Doris and the two began with a couple of wets before proceeding to the pictures. Jim and Lynn were also there, although they left the bioscope early. Bill and Doris continued their evening together and had a good time.\n\nAt some point during the evening an argument developed with an English officer and came close to turning into a fight. Bill believed that, had he been on his own, it probably would have done. He eventually returned aboard at about 12.30 a.m."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 17 February 1942",
    "It was bitterly cold today, and both Bill and Bert were suffering from bad colds. The combination of the weather and illness had left Bill thoroughly tired.\n\nRather than going ashore for another evening out, he decided to turn in early and get some rest."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 18 February 1942",
    "Bill met Doris again at six o’clock in the evening. Finding one another in Glasgow was evidently not always straightforward, and it took about an hour of searching before they were together.\n\nThey proceeded to the Grant Arms Hotel and later went to the pictures. After spending the evening together, Bill caught the 11 p.m. train back and was aboard Tui by about 12.30 a.m."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 19 February 1942",
    "Bert was not aboard, and Bill heard that he had been in a fight. During the altercation, some Pongo had pushed a broken bottle into Bert’s face, leaving a wound that required seven stitches. By two o’clock that afternoon Bert was still ashore.\n\nBill visited the dentist while preparations were being made to go out on convoy work. More troops were being embarked aboard the transports. The weather was fine, although it remained as cold as hell.\n\nBert eventually returned aboard with his face bandaged. Tui moved out into the stream to test her compass, and Bill hoped they would not be going far. He wanted to get ashore and look for the man who had injured Bert. Don’t blame me Mum.\n\nAfter some mucking around, Tui eventually had to sail. She left the harbour at 10 p.m. in calm, slightly misty conditions."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 20 February 1942",
    "The sea remained calm and the day was sunny, although Bill was tired as hell. Even after coming off watch there was work to be done, with the decks needing to be scrubbed. He finally turned in at 3.20 p.m., sleeping until 6.45 p.m., when he got up, had supper and prepared to go back on duty at 9 p.m.\n\nBert was a little better, although his injured face was still sore after the previous day’s events. Land was once again in sight, and Bill hoped they would be back in port by Tuesday.\n\nBill Jordan, the N.Z. Commissioner, was due to visit them before leaving for home. With the sea calm and the weather holding, Bill hoped they would have conditions like these for the coming trip. His thoughts then turned homeward as he wondered what everyone there was doing."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 21 February 1942",
    "The weather had changed dramatically overnight. Snow was falling and the wind was blowing like hell, giving them a bit of a hiding after the calm, sunny conditions of the previous day.\n\nBert was a lot better, his injured face continuing to improve. Bill was less impressed with the food, with cured beef being served yet again and seemingly becoming a regular part of their diet.\n\nThey were due back in port at 11 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 22 February 1942",
    "It was a good, calm day, and much of the morning was spent cleaning up in preparation for the Commissioner’s visit. They secured at 11.30 a.m., bringing the latest period at sea to an end.\n\nBill spent some time dhobying and remained aboard for the rest of the day. It was cold out on deck, and he turned in at 10.15 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 23 February 1942",
    "Another good day, although it remained cold as hell. There was an inoculation that day, during which Bill managed to put something across the doctor.\n\nBill received a letter from a Glasgow girl who wanted to buy him a ring, an offer that certainly caught his attention. Cecil paid them a visit, while Bert had gone ashore and was now a lot better following his injury. Bill spent some of the day writing letters to his girlfriends."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 24 February 1942",
    "The good weather continued, with the day spent cleaning up in preparation for Bill Jordan’s visit.\n\nBill went ashore that evening to a dance at Craigburn. The dance was not bad, although the woman he was with was not so hot. He could have taken a Wren home, but instead returned aboard by 11.45 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 25 February 1942",
    "After a final clean-up, the High Commissioner came aboard at 1.15 p.m. Bill took five photographs of him and thought he was a decent sort of chap. There was an inspection followed by a speech, after which the men gave him three cheers.\n\nLater, Bill sneaked ashore to the pictures, only to find himself sitting beside the coxswain — hell. He was back aboard by 9.50 p.m. and hoped they would be leaving very soon.\n\nSo long Mum & Leon. All the Best."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 26 February 1942",
    "Bill had forgotten to mention that he had cut the captain’s hair for the High Commissioner’s visit, as well as Lieutenant Palmer’s, who was from Auckland. The last three or four days sure had been busy for him.\n\nBill Jordan went ashore, and the sweeping gear was checked over prior to going out. Bert was sent to hospital, his injured face now rather serious, and Bill hoped he would not miss the ship. There was a buzz that they would be leaving the following week.\n\nBill went ashore to a dance that evening and had a pretty good time, but was thirty minutes adrift getting back aboard."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 27 February 1942",
    "They went to sea in rain and rigged the sweeping gear, but conditions were too rough to begin sweeping.\n\nWith Bert still in hospital, Bill spoke to the captain about remaining behind if his brother was unable to come with them. The news was good — Bert would make it all right and would not be left behind.\n\nThe weather improved as the day went on, and they anchored for the night at Rothesay Bay, where Bill kept anchor watch."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 28 February 1942",
    "They carried out minesweeping during the day and dropped two depth charges, with the weather not too bad. They were back in port by 4.30 p.m., where Bert came aboard for a visit while still away from the ship receiving treatment.\n\nBill went ashore at 5.30 p.m. and bought a few souvenirs before heading to the Queen’s Hotel for a few wets. From there he went to a dance and became pretty well hooped. He saw a fairy home before returning aboard just after midnight.\n\nIt was only then that Bill realised he was a year older."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 1 March 1942",
    "Bill had a thick head that morning after the previous night’s drinking and thought he might lay off the hops for a while. He went into Glasgow looking for the cur who had cut Bert’s throat, but had no luck finding him.\n\nHe was back aboard by 8 p.m. and left the shielas alone for the night. The latest buzz was that they would be leaving for home on Tuesday. Now that the time had finally come, Bill felt rather sorry to be leaving. Perhaps, he thought, it was the love-bug at work again."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 2 March 1942",
    "It was still cold, and Bill spent some of the day fixing the minesweeping davits. Bert came aboard from hospital, a welcome sign after the concern over his injured face during the previous few days.\n\nBill went ashore at 4.30 p.m. and headed to the pictures to see Andy Hardy Steps Out. Afterwards he met a couple of fairies and went with them to a party — hell what a party, ha! ha! He eventually walked back to the ship, the journey taking him an hour."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 3 March 1942",
    "The wind was blowing up again, and Bill no longer thought they would be leaving that week. He was on duty and spent some of the day dhobying, as well as cutting the First Lieutenant’s hair. He also wrote a few letters before turning in at 10.30 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 4 March 1942",
    "It was raining like the devil, but Bill had a date and went ashore at 6 p.m. He met up with a cobber, but the sheilas they were expecting did not turn up. They had a couple of wets instead, and Bill made a date with the barmaid for Sunday.\n\nHe was back aboard by 10 p.m. and turned in for the night."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 5 March 1942",
    "It was snowing and as cold as hell, with the sea rough enough for a line to break while they were trying to pull the ship in. They secured for going to sea.\n\nBill was on duty and spent some of the day dhobying and cutting hair. He received a Christmas card from Frank, while Bert had a letter from a friend in New Zealand. From the news in the letter, it seemed they were going to extremes with the blackouts back home.\n\nBill turned in at 10.30 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 6 March 1942",
    "Bill gave the sweeping store a good clean while snow fell heavily outside. He went ashore at 4.50 p.m., with the snow still falling and the weather bitterly cold. He bought some biscuits for the men and had to present a silver fern to the girl behind the counter.\n\nHe went ashore again at 7.30 p.m., giving a tin of pudding and tongue to the chap on the gate. After a couple of wets and seeing the barmaid, Bill went to the Craigburn dance and had a good time.\n\nA blizzard was blowing as he made his way back to the ship, and the snow was over the tops of his seaboots. He had never seen so much snow. Once aboard, he made some supper and turned in at 12.30 a.m."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 7 March 1942",
    "The snow continued, although it was not falling quite as heavily. Bill and the others had a darn good snow fight before the snow began coming down heavily again that evening.\n\nStores were brought aboard during the day. Bill was on duty that night and did a little dhobying before settling down to read. Rather tired, he turned in at 10 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 8 March 1942",
    "Bill began the day clearing snow from the decks. A snow fight broke out with the crew of Scarba, with even their skipper joining in. Divisions and a church service were held aboard, but there was no leave — they were going to sea.\n\nThey sailed at 4.30 p.m. with a convoy that included Rangitata. As they headed out, Bill saw ships laden with troops making their way into port. The sun was shining, and the snow covering the surrounding hills and mountains made a lovely picture.\n\nThe night remained clear, with the moon shining brightly. They passed a destroyer and a submarine before Bill finally turned in at 3 a.m., tired and cold."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 9 March 1942",
    "It was a lovely day with the sun shining, and Bill enjoyed a sun bath. During the day they carried out gun drill, abandon ship drill and fire stations. There were some fair-sized ships in the convoy.\n\nA few aircraft appeared overhead, but each time they proved to be friendly. Whenever one came into view, all eyes were focused on it. The men were issued with skin jackets to help keep them warm.\n\nSome of the janky skippers of the other ships gave a little trouble at first. All five New Zealand ships were with the convoy.\n\nThe night was calm and clear. Bill was up again at 3 a.m."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 10 March 1942",
    "They left the convoy at Loch H and proceeded back, the night remaining clear apart from one shower. The five ships carried out fleet manoeuvres, but Scarba’s steering gear jammed and delayed them for two hours.\n\nBill spent some of the day painting the deck and cutting hair. He went on duty again at 9 p.m. and was already tired. The latest buzz was that they would be leaving in about ten days, although Bill still wondered when they would finally get away. He guessed that Bert would have his birthday at sea.\n\nThere were plenty of ships about that night, so Bill had to keep a careful lookout. At midnight he was relieved from duty and could turn in."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 11 March 1942",
    "The morning was clear, although the sun was not yet up. Despite the continuing cold, Bill found it a real pleasure to be at sea on this trip. They passed the Isle of Craig at 8 a.m. and would soon be back in harbour.\n\nAn aircraft carrier which Bill identified as Glorious passed them. They oiled up before entering harbour. Bill was on duty and spent some of his time dhobying.\n\nSome of the boys came aboard drunk, bringing a clock, photographs and another item Bill’s handwriting leaves unclear. They were certainly drunk. Bill had a bath before turning in. So-long."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 12 March 1942",
    "Bill scrubbed the decks, followed by an awful breakfast. There was another disagreement over the coxswain. Not much was happening during the day, and Bill spent some time painting. Bert was serving as Quartermaster and did not like it.\n\nThat evening Bill went into Glasgow to meet a fairy. He had a pretty good time and found there was some great talent around, particularly Leen — ahem!\n\nUnable to get back aboard, Bill was lucky enough to obtain the last available bed at the 7 Seas Club for the night."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 13 March 1942",
    "Bill arrived aboard five minutes adrift, just as the ship was pulling out to move to another berth. He went to the dentist during the day and had another tooth filled.\n\nRather tired that evening, Bill did a little ironing before turning in. Bert was now solely in charge of the washing of the 4-inch gun and was glad to be rid of the Quartermaster’s job."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 14 March 1942",
    "Everything was made secure for sea, with a strong buzz that they would finally be leaving the following day.\n\nBill went ashore at 5 p.m. and bought bread, milk and biscuits. He went ashore again at 6 p.m. and had a couple of wets — well, a few. He met the barmaid and a cobber and had a great time, the best he had enjoyed in Greenock.\n\nBill made a date with the barmaid for 6.30 p.m. the following evening and hoped it would be fulfilled. He thought she was a great fairy and good sport."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 15 March 1942",
    "Bill gave the upper deck a once-over and helped clean the lower decks. The time had finally come. They were leaving that day to begin the long trip home.\n\nA farewell church service was held for the crews of all five New Zealand ships. Reports of storms in the Atlantic were less encouraging, and Bill hoped they would have a decent trip. He wrote his final letters to friends in England before departure.\n\nThey left Greenock jetty at 4.45 p.m. and proceeded to Rothesay Bay, where they anchored until midnight. From there they would head for the rendezvous off the coast of Ireland and join a convoy of about forty ships, accompanied by the five New Zealand vessels, destroyers and corvettes.\n\nBill was on anchor duty until 9 p.m. and was due back on watch as lookout at 3 a.m. He turned in hoping for a good run of fine weather. Cheerio."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 16 March 1942",
    "The weather was not too good, with fog and poor visibility. They proceeded with the convoy, picking up more ships at sea from Liverpool before heading to the rendezvous for the remainder coming from Belfast. There were twelve escorting vessels.\n\nThe weather improved a little as they headed west. Now that Bill was finally on his way home, he found that he did not feel particularly thrilled about it. The first port was said to be St John’s, Newfoundland.\n\nDuring the passage they attacked a submarine, although Bill did not know whether it had been sunk or managed to get away. He came off watch at midnight and turned in while the hunt for the submarine was still underway."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 17 March 1942",
    "It was a great day with the sun shining, although a big swell had the ship rolling like hell. Killegray collided with a merchant ship, but there was no damage.\n\nBy sundown it had become overcast and the barometer was falling. Bill hoped they were not in for a rough spin. One chap was seasick, while Bert was not at his best and neither were a few of the others.\n\nBill turned in before going on duty again at midnight. Supper had been salmon and an apology for soup. He never wanted to see salmon or corned beef again for ages.\n\nHell, she was rolling."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 18 March 1942",
    "It was a clear night, and more depth charges were dropped during the morning. The weather developed into a lovely day, allowing Bill to have another sun bath.\n\nThe convoy was still making only 7½ knots, with one Greek ship unable to keep up with the others. They carried out gun practice, fire stations and other drills during the day.\n\nBill went on duty again at 6 p.m. The ship was still rolling in the Atlantic swell."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 19 March 1942",
    "Hells bells, the Atlantic certainly knew how to roll. The sea was rough, and an aircraft sighted that morning kept everyone in suspense until it dropped recognition lights and showed itself to be friendly.\n\nThe weather remained showery, with a few green ones coming over the bows. One nearly caught Bill, though he managed to avoid getting wet. The wind dropped a little later in the day. Bill bet they would have the same weather again tomorrow and hoped he would lose the bet.\n\nHe turned in tired and not feeling too good."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 20 March 1942",
    "Bill lost his tot, but with the weather good he was pleased to have lost the bet. More depth charges had been dropped during the night.\n\nThe improvement did not last. A gale began to blow like hell, and Bill guessed they were in for it."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 21 March 1942",
    "They were taking seas aboard everywhere. The decks were leaking, the cabins were wet and the mess deck was wet, while the upper deck was awash in about six inches of water much of the time.\n\nBill was properly browned off with the relentless conditions and wondered what a calm sea felt like."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 22 March 1942",
    "There was a church service that day. It was still blowing, although not quite so badly, and they were now halfway to St John’s.\n\nBread was running short, leaving meals largely consisting of corned dog or salmon — hellish fare as far as Bill was concerned. After days of rough weather, the night brought a welcome change and was lovely and clear.\n\nBill remained on duty until midnight. Afterwards, he raided the galley."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 23 March 1942",
    "It was a great day with the sun shining, although there was still a swell running. There was a mishap in the galley when the water tank burst.\n\nBill cut the skipper’s hair and spent some of the day working on the ship’s magazine.\n\nThe weather remained good into the evening, with a clear night."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 24 March 1942",
    "It was a lovely day, the best they had experienced on the voyage so far. Bill did some hair-cutting and took advantage of the fine weather to sun-bathe.\n\nThe water aboard was no good, and three of the boys were sick. Meals were little better, with corned dog for both breakfast and dinner. Fortunately, there were big eats for supper — thank goodness."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 25 March 1942",
    "They were being checked by a submarine, and extra lookouts were stationed. It was cold as the devil, with fog and a fairly rough sea making conditions even more uncomfortable.\n\nAt least the food had improved and was pretty good that day.\n\nBill went on duty from 9 p.m. until midnight. It was a very cold watch."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 26 March 1942",
    "Bill went on duty at 6 a.m. It was a great day, and the five New Zealand ships left the convoy to proceed on their own towards St John’s. They expected to arrive the following night.\n\nDuring the day they carried out action stations, gun drills and other exercises. There was another submarine alert before the weather deteriorated badly, with snow beginning to fall and the wind starting to blow."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 27 March 1942",
    "Hells bells, what wind and snow. The sea was rough and they were being tossed like a cork. Their arrival was now put back until the following day.\n\nVisibility was poor and the decks were leaking everywhere, leaving everything wet. These were about the worst conditions Bill had encountered yet."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 28 March 1942",
    "The sun began to come out, the wind died down and the snow finally stopped. They entered St John’s at 1.30 p.m., finding calm water once inside the harbour after the severe conditions of the previous days.\n\nShore leave was granted, but Bill had no local money and it cost 20 cents to get ashore. They were told that English silver was accepted. Bert went ashore and had a fair time. Bill found things expensive."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 29 March 1942",
    "The sun was shining and the weather was good, although snow still lay all around. Bill had thought they had seen the last of it and was glad to be safely in port.\n\nThe men were issued with ten dollars. Bert was dying to get ashore again. Bill had loaned him a few bob the previous night.\n\nThe Atlantic crossing had taken them thirteen days and brought just about every kind of weather. The latest buzz was that they would leave again on Wednesday."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 30 March 1942",
    "After a general clean-up, the sun was shining and it was quiet. Bill went ashore at 4.30 p.m., had an ice-cream milkshake, and bought silk stockings and a box of chocolates. He had a good meal followed by apple pie.\n\nA few wets followed, although the local beer was not too good. He went into a low dive where the beer was particularly vile, with the price or quantity given as 10c. 4pts. The girls were not so hot either.\n\nThere was little entertainment apart from the pictures. Bill returned aboard at 12.30 a.m., with the trip between the ship and shore costing twenty cents each way."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 31 March 1942",
    "Bill went aboard Scarba for log line, then did a little shopping, buying a photograph album and some books for a youngster. They had heard that three submarines had been chasing them during the Atlantic crossing and that one of them had been got.\n\nIt was snowing like hell and cold. Bill did some dhobying and had been made Leading Seaman. He also did some hair-cutting. The water aboard the ship had frozen again. After resting for a while, he turned in at 10 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 1 April 1942",
    "The snow had stopped and the sun was shining, although it was still cold. Bill intended to go ashore and spend all the Canadian money he had left. Dinner aboard that day was awful.\n\nHe went ashore at 8 p.m. and attended a dance at the YMCA, but thought the dancing was awful. A big cab took no one home, although Bill reckoned he could have. Bert was out with a cobber and two sheilas."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 2 April 1942",
    "It was snowing like the devil, with a gale expected and talk of putting extra lines out. Bill was on duty that night and stayed aboard.\n\nThe latest buzz was that they would sail the following day. Bill had collected a few souvenirs during his stay in St John’s, including stockings, cookies, a cover and children’s books."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 3 April 1942",
    "The water was frozen and there was ice all around, although the sun was shining. They were due to sail that day and the men were issued with woollen caps, books and magazines.\n\nThey left the jetty at 10.30 a.m. Once outside there was a good swell running and it was cold as hell."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 4 April 1942",
    "The weather was getting warmer and the sun was shining, with the air temperature at 35°F and the water at 32°F. They had action stations and gun drill while wearing gas masks.\n\nDinner was not bad, although there was no sweet, while supper was terrible. By later in the day the temperature had risen to 42°F, with the water at 34°F. The wind had dropped a little, although there was still a swell running.\n\nBill was due on duty again at midnight and hoped it would be warmer. Everyone was properly browned off with the cold. A couple of aircraft passed overhead, and one caused a momentary scare when they thought it might be a Jerry while they were observing it. They also passed a fairly large convoy during the day. The evening ended with a great sunset."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 5 April 1942",
    "The day was dull with rain at times. They lost one of their mascots, the dog.\n\nDuring the passage they left the Labrador Current and entered the Gulf Stream. The temperature rose seven degrees in only half an hour, making conditions 100 per cent better for Bill while he was on watch.\n\nA church service was held during the day."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 6 April 1942",
    "It was a great day, warm and calm. Gun drill and the usual exercises were carried out, while the improving weather had the boys discarding their jerseys and other warm clothing.\n\nWreckage was seen floating in the sea, along with a dinghy. It appeared that a ship must have gone down.\n\nAfter the cold and rough conditions of the previous weeks, it was real good to have decent weather again."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 7 April 1942",
    "Bill sunbathed, cut hair and helped paint the ship. He also spent time on lookout in the crow’s nest and studied for an examination.\n\nIt was a good moonlit night. They expected to be in the following afternoon, and Bill was delighted at the prospect of shore leave. Wacko!"
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 8 April 1942",
    "Land was sighted at 11 a.m. A pilot came aboard at St George’s and took them through to Somerset Dockyard, where they berthed at 4 p.m. Bill was immediately impressed by Bermuda. It was a great place and very attractive, with remarkably blue water.\n\nHell, Bill had duty that night. Seeing lights without the familiar blackout was a real treat. The starboard watch went ashore and came back aboard drunk, some showing bruises and cuts after falling over."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 9 April 1942",
    "Bill spent part of the day painting the ship in the sun, which was very hot. It was also a make-and-mend day. He then went across the water to Hamilton, finding it a very picturesque place with wonderful scenery and sights he would never forget. There were no cars to be seen, only bicycles and horse-drawn carts, among the colourful buildings.\n\nHe bought postcards, a silk bathing suit and films, then had supper before going dancing and having a few wets. Beer came in cans, and he also drank Canadian Club cocktails. He met a couple of fairies and had a fight before eventually sleeping ashore.\n\nIt had been a wonderful night and a great time. Bill also made friends with a couple of American sailors and had spent the evening ashore with Trapper Dan."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 10 April 1942",
    "Bill sunbathed and continued painting the ship. He received the bird over his new bathing suit, cut more hair and did some dhobying. Bert’s ankle was about the same, and he was raving to get ashore.\n\nThey could now sleep on deck, where it was lovely and cool. News had reached them of the sinking of HMS Dorsetshire and HMS Cornwall. Hell, when were they going to retaliate? They had also heard of the loss of HMS Hermes.\n\nOne good thing was that the blackout had not started in Bermuda yet. Only three were left aboard — Bert, Bill and a cobber. Bill turned in with a final so-long."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 11 April 1942",
    "Bill spent more of the morning painting the ship and went swimming before dinner. Leave began at 1 p.m., giving him a chance to have a good look around town and buy some souvenirs.\n\nHe stopped for ice cream and apple pie and saw a fight while ashore. Later he went dancing, which was not bad, and finished the evening with a good supper."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 12 April 1942",
    "Bill was back aboard at 8 a.m. and discovered that Bert was in hospital having his foot X-rayed. He would curse when he was told that some luscious fairies had been asking after him and were apparently prepared to buy him milkshakes all day.\n\nThere was a church service that morning, with Bill reckoning the girls in the choir were the best he had seen in Bermuda. The men also took part in a march-past and salute before the Commodore. Bill turned in during the afternoon and was on duty that day."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 13 April 1942",
    "Bill was still painting the ship, and an awning was rigged for a party aboard that night. There was a little rain during the day.\n\nHe went ashore at 4.15 p.m. and had his photograph taken on movie film. He strolled around town and bought some cards of the island. After having a wet, he met a civie and went home with him, where they had a few more wets and supper. The visit gave Bill a chance to see a good deal more of the island.\n\nHe was back aboard at 12.10 a.m. and turned in tired."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 14 April 1942",
    "Bill spent more of the day painting the hull and sunbathing. There was not much for dinner, and his cash was beginning to run low. He was unable to spend his English £1, which made matters worse.\n\nSomething was wrong with the engines, and it now looked as though they would probably be staying in Bermuda longer than expected. Bill was on duty that night and cut some hair, while the boys were over on the other ships.\n\nHe turned in at 10.30 p.m. and slept on deck."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 15 April 1942",
    "Bill spent the morning painting. A children’s party had been arranged aboard for the afternoon, and from 4 until 6 p.m. about forty children came aboard. Everyone had a good time.\n\nLater Bill went ashore with Trapper Dan, who ran into some sailors from USS Savannah whom he had met in Auckland the previous year. They had a few wets together. A few more drinks followed before they went to a private dance, and Bill also had a ride in a “cabby.”\n\nBy then he was pretty well pickled. When it came time to turn in, he had to climb fences to reach their bed after being locked out. During the evening he had also nearly become involved in two fights."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 16 April 1942",
    "The clocks went forward an hour, leaving Bill with only three and a half hours’ sleep. He was rather hazy in the head and bought some lemonade after having had a lot of revelries.\n\nThankfully it was make and mend, giving him a more relaxed day after the previous night. He went swimming, had a good supper and turned in early. Bert was still in hospital and had not missed much during his time there."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 17 April 1942",
    "There was a little rain in the morning, but Bill continued painting the hull. Later he went ashore to a dance and had a swell time. By the end of the evening he was pretty well hooped.\n\nHe finally made it back aboard at 2.30 a.m., tired out after the late night."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 18 April 1942",
    "Bert came out of hospital at 10 a.m. and was already raving to go ashore. Bill was on duty for the day and spent some time playing the gramophone, the music bringing back memories of home.\n\nHe was darned tired and turned in early. With no opportunity to send mail home, his thoughts again turned to New Zealand. Never mind—he hoped to see everyone there in another three or four months."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 19 April 1942",
    "It was raining like hell as the ship prepared for an Admiral’s inspection. Later Bill hired a bicycle and went touring around the island, taking photographs along the way and having a swell time.\n\nWhile out he met a fine girl and her friend whom he had met at the dance. It was his last day in Bermuda."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 20 April 1942",
    "They left Bermuda at 9.55 a.m. bound for Jamaica. It was a great day, and Bill saw a two-finned shark swim by as they headed out to sea.\n\nHe had a sleep during the afternoon. The watchkeeping routine was now different from that used on the voyages to England and Canada. Bill was also thinking about home again and hoped to drop a line from the next port if possible."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 21 April 1942",
    "They were carrying another three passengers on the voyage to Jamaica, while one man had been left behind in Bermuda suffering from yellow jaundice.\n\nIt had rained like hell during the night, but the weather was great during the day and Bill enjoyed a siesta in the sun. Bert’s foot was still not quite better. There was gun drill and other routine work, while Bill remained undecided about whether to go for his exam. He signed off until tomorrow."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 22 April 1942",
    "It rained like hell for a couple of hours before the weather cleared enough for Bill to spend some time sunbathing. During the day they fired two rounds from the 4-inch gun. Bill cut Lieutenant Frankham’s hair as well as the skipper’s, alongside the usual shipboard routine.\n\nAt 11 p.m. they pinged a submarine. The alert continued through the night before finally clearing at 9 a.m. the following morning."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 23 April 1942",
    "It was hot as hell when Bill went on watch at 9 a.m. The food was awful. There was not enough bread, and what remained was mouldy and stank. Before long they had run out of bread altogether.\n\nBill’s gum was also giving him trouble, which he put down to the lack of proper food. The heat continued throughout the day, hot as the devil."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 24 April 1942",
    "There was still no bread aboard. The cook managed to make some by dinnertime, but there was only one loaf to last ten men for twenty-four hours.\n\nThe waters were infested with submarines, and depth charges were dropped three times during the day. Such incidents were becoming common now. Bill cut the First Lieutenant’s hair and the skipper’s, as well as the hair of some of the crew. He turned in around dhobying time.\n\nBert’s foot was just about O.K. They passed Mayaguana Island during the morning and were running towards another island during the afternoon."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 25 April 1942",
    "Bill had forgotten that it was Anzac Day. They passed Cuba during the morning and later Navassa Island, while Bill managed a little sunbathing along the way.\n\nIt was hot as Hades as they continued towards Jamaica, where they were due to arrive the following morning. Bill had also decided that he was still going to try for his higher rating."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 26 April 1942",
    "They arrived in Jamaica at 9.15 a.m., oiled and then proceeded to their berth. There was fruit in abundance, and Bill had his first banana for more than twelve months. It was very hot.\n\nWomen crowded onto the jetty offering to do the sailors’ dhobying, and Bill was more than happy to have his washing done. He was on duty that day, while some of the boys from the other ships were already blotto. Jamaica certainly seemed a great place, and Bill was keen to buy some souvenirs.\n\nBert was out on patrol. Bill had offered to do the patrol himself, but nothing doing. Tropical rig was now being worn. Bill had made up his mind to go for the exam, but for the moment he needed to turn in—he had the middle watch that night."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 27 April 1942",
    "Bill went ashore at 1 p.m. and immediately noticed some fair efforts. What a place. He and some of the others hopped into a car with a group of sheilas, and before long he was having a few wets.\n\nThe day developed into quite an experience. Jamaica was already the best place Bill felt he had visited, and he wished Leon and Harry could have been there with him. He visited some local bacchantes but did not indulge, although by then he was well hooped up. He bought a few souvenirs and altogether had a real swell time.\n\nBert went exploring for bacchantes around St Andrew and another place Bill names as “La Pavert.”"
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 28 April 1942",
    "The people ashore were continuing to turn it on for the visiting sailors, with invitations to swimming baths, a cigar factory, the Wembley Hotel and a number of other places.\n\nBill visited a cigar factory and thoroughly enjoyed himself there. He bought something before strolling around and eventually making his way to Palm Beach. “What a place,” he wrote, adding an amused “ahem!” and remarking that there was a great deal he could tell about it.\n\nHe started back at about 9.30 p.m. and stopped at the Blue Peter on the way, an experience that earned another emphatic “whew!” in the journal.\n\nBill also traded his lighter during the outing. It had been another full day ashore and one that clearly gave him plenty to write about."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 29 April 1942",
    "Bill was tired and broke after the previous night's adventures, and with work to do that afternoon he thought he might stay aboard. Fifty bunches of bananas were obtained for the crew, an impressive supply after Bill had tasted his first banana in more than twelve months only three days earlier.\n\nIt was still hot as hell. Bert had gone ashore—for what? ha! ha!\n\nThey were packing up ready to leave the following day. Despite being tired and short of money, Bill decided he might hop ashore once more and spend his last 1/3 in Jamaican money."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 30 April 1942",
    "Bill was up at 5 a.m., and they left the jetty an hour later, bound for Colón. The heat was hellish, with sweat dripping onto the ink as he wrote. After the last few busy days in Jamaica and the early start that morning, he was very tired.\n\nThere was now a new mascot aboard—a golden-haired spaniel. As they headed west across the Caribbean towards Panama, Bill noticed that another month had come to an end."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 1 May 1942",
    "It was a great moonlit morning, and Bill saw the Southern Cross. He had an evaluative that day. It was very hot, and he painted the ship and carried on with the usual routine before taking a siesta during the afternoon."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 2 May 1942",
    "It was doubtful whether they would reach Colón that night. Sanda was having trouble, and the ships were reduced to simply cruising along at about six knots. Hells bells.\n\nIt was doubtful whether they would arrive that night. Sanda was having trouble, and they were only making about six knots. Hells bells. They passed a few ships, and it rained during the night."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 3 May 1942",
    "They arrived off Colón at 11.30 a.m., where a pilot came aboard along with a docker. The ship dropped anchor while they waited for further orders. To Bill's disappointment, they were to remain out in the stream until the following day."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 4 May 1942",
    "There were plenty of aircraft around Colón. They tied alongside at 10 a.m. and took on oil. Bill was on duty that day, but there were good reports from the boys coming back aboard, who had been made welcome ashore. Bill's own turn ashore would come the following day.\n\nHe had become browned off with his Quartermaster duties. Bert was a guest at the canteen, and there were large crabs on the jetty."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 5 May 1942",
    "There were plenty of sandflies and mosquitoes. Bill palled up with a couple of sergeants who invited him to dinner, and he had a great time with them and some officers, whom he found to be great fellows.\n\nHe bought some souvenirs and went to the nightclubs, where he saw a striptease. He also had his photograph taken, and later met some of the ship's officers and had a wet with them. Bill evidently made the most of his day ashore, eventually overstaying his leave by four hours."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 6 May 1942",
    "Bill was very tired that morning after getting very little sleep. He carried on with the usual routine aboard ship.\n\nSome of the friends he had made ashore came aboard to visit, and Bill gave them souvenir coins, a badge and other small keepsakes. He turned in at 10.10 p.m. to get some sleep before going on duty at midnight."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 7 May 1942",
    "It rained like the devil that morning. Bill was having dinner at a barracks before going ashore at 1 p.m. He invited the sergeants over to share his tot and thought how much Leon would have enjoyed himself there.\n\nThere were farewells to the soldiers before Bill headed into town again. He visited the nightclubs and saw some great striptease dances. Wacko!\n\nBill returned aboard an hour adrift, while Bert was three hours late. Bill shaved off his beard."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 8 May 1942",
    "They left the jetty at 8 a.m. and spent the day travelling through the Panama Canal, finally berthing at Balboa on the Pacific side at 6 p.m.\n\nBy then Bill was thoroughly chocka with the ship and would be glad when he was drafted off her. He wished he had stayed in England and felt that he had not been given a fair deal."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 9 May 1942",
    "It was raining like hell. The men were issued free ice cream, while Bill spent some of the day cutting hair before settling down for a siesta. He found some of the local people to be pro-Nazi.\n\nNews also reached them of a great naval battle off Australia."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 10 May 1942",
    "They left the jetty at noon, beginning what Bill regarded as the second lap of their journey home. Rain fell heavily as they departed, although he found the surroundings rather picturesque.\n\nBill was chocka with the voyage and would be glad when the trip was finally over. The ship had now lost its cat, leaving the cocker spaniel as the remaining mascot. As they headed out into the Pacific, the riding lights were switched on."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 11 May 1942",
    "The weather was a little colder as they continued into the Pacific, with more rain during the day. Bill was tired. At some point the ship went to action stations."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 12 May 1942",
    "It was Bert's birthday. The rain continued, although the weather remained warm. At some point during the day Bill had a fight."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 13 May 1942",
    "Mexico came into sight at 5 a.m. During the day Bill saw a shoal of sharks and drifting logs.\n\nScarba came alongside with half a day's supply of tuna, although the cook managed to spoil it. The weather was beginning to look better."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 14 May 1942",
    "The day was dull but warm. Bill was suffering from heat rash and mosquito bites and remained thoroughly chocka with life aboard, regarding it as the worst ship he had served on."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 15 May 1942",
    "It was very hot, and Bill spent some time sunbathing between a few showers. He saw a turtle during the day, with plenty more turtles and porpoises appearing around the ships as the passage continued.\n\nA sick rating came aboard while they were out in the Pacific. Otherwise, it was the usual routine."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 16 May 1942",
    "There were many sharks and tuna around the ship as they continued northwards through the eastern Pacific. The heat remained intense, with Bill perspiring like hell.\n\nThe Captain carried out his rounds during the day."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 17 May 1942",
    "The day began with divisions and church. More turtles and sharks were seen around the ship, and Bill later spent some time sunbathing and painting the ship.\n\nDiscontent aboard was growing, with more snivelling among the men. Bill was increasingly wondering whether he would ever get off the ship. Food was also becoming scarce."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 18 May 1942",
    "Plenty of porpoises were around again as Bill continued painting the ship. Trouble developed aboard Scarba when her coal caught fire.\n\nSick ratings had to be moved, and Bill took photographs during the day."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 19 May 1942",
    "Bill spent more of the day painting the ship, although his eyes were bloodshot. Food remained in short supply. He cut hair during the day, while more porpoises appeared around the ship.\n\nThe weather had been great so far, although it became a little colder that night. They were due to reach port a week from today."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 20 May 1942",
    "Bill continued painting the ship as the voyage northwards continued. Food supplies were now pretty low, with the men receiving only three pieces of bread over twenty-four hours. Despite the shortage, Bill felt that the food had not been bad on this trip overall."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 21 May 1942",
    "The sea was choppy during the day, and Bill continued painting the ship. It was especially still at times and rather cool. He asked for $50 for the next port.\n\nBy the time he went on watch from 9 p.m. until midnight, it was cold as hell."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 22 May 1942",
    "It was still darn cold. Bill cleaned the mess deck, continued painting the ship and cut hair during the day.\n\nAt 4 p.m. they sighted land on the Southern California coast."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 23 May 1942",
    "It was still cold. Bill caught a tuna on a line, which went to the wardroom. He had to provide trays, while a member of Sanda's crew was being shipped aboard."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 24 May 1942",
    "The day began with divisions and church. Bill remained thoroughly dissatisfied with life aboard—what a ship! It was cold as the devil, and he was on duty from midday."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 25 May 1942",
    "Bill expected to sight land during the day. It was a little warmer, with the sun shining, and they had the radio on with some good music to listen to.\n\nWith their arrival now close, Bill was already planning what he would do ashore and hoping for a good time."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 26 May 1942",
    "They reached San Pedro at 8.30 a.m. to a warm, sunny day, and Bill's first impression was that the place seemed O.K. He had duty aboard ship that day, but received an invitation to a movie party and had a great time at a wonderful performance.\n\nLater he went to the Del Rio night club, where he was particularly impressed by the girls—the most beautiful he had seen. He returned aboard forty-five minutes adrift and went on watch at midnight. The night became considerably more exciting when Bill had to draw his revolver on a man and fire. Afterwards, he had coffee and cakes in the C.P.O.'s mess.\n\nHe finished the day's entry with three names: Georgia Carol, Susan Foster and Joan Blake."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 27 May 1942",
    "Bill went ashore at 4 p.m. and had something to eat at the canteen, where he found the men there to be swell guys. From San Pedro he travelled into Los Angeles and visited the nightclubs, having a swell time.\n\nBert clicked with a girl who had a 1942 Chevrolet, giving them a car for their night out. During the evening Bill injured his back again—darn it—but there were already plenty of tales to tell from their time in California. They also made their way to Hollywood."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 10 June 1942",
    "Bill had been having such a swell time with the New Zealand people he met in California that he had been too busy to keep up his diary. Looking back over the missing fortnight, he regarded this as the best port he had visited and felt that he had enjoyed some of the swellest times of the entire voyage.\n\nAmong the people who had made his stay memorable were Hine, Wayne, Virgie, Lecter, Lil, Dave and Mum."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 11 June 1942",
    "They left San Pedro at 7 a.m., but had to return to pick up a rating from a tanker for one of the other ships. Killegray also had three ratings adrift.\n\nBill was tired by the end of the day and turned in."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 12 June 1942",
    "The weather was dull and the sea a little choppy, with no sun showing. Bill's thoughts were still on Long Beach and the time he had enjoyed there. After the busy days and nights ashore in California, he was still catching up on his sleep."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 13 June 1942",
    "They picked up the rest of the convoy as the voyage continued across the North Pacific. The weather was still dull, although Bill was beginning to feel a little better.\n\nHis thoughts remained with California and the good times he had enjoyed there. He wished he could be back and hoped that one day he would return."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 14 June 1942",
    "The usual Sunday divisions and church were held. Bill was thoroughly chocka with the old man. The weather remained dull, and the night was black as the devil."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 15 June 1942",
    "The ship went to action stations during the day. The weather remained dull, although the sun broke through for about three hours. There was still a roll on from the swell. Bill wrote letters back to California, while the weather was becoming warmer again."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 16 June 1942",
    "It was warm now, although the weather remained dull and the nights were black. The gun fuses were tried but proved no good, leaving Bill and the others to clean the shells and re-fuse them.\n\nBill slept throughout the afternoon. Bread was again in short supply."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 17 June 1942",
    "The ship went to action stations, and Bill spent part of the day cleaning the guns. Bread was now being rationed, while some of the meat had gone rotten, leaving the men to go without meals.\n\nCrabs had also broken out aboard ship, and Bill hoped he would not catch them. There was not much good food to be had."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 18 June 1942",
    "It was a great day, with the sun shining after the recent stretch of dull weather. Bill took part in gun-cleaning drill during the day.\n\nThe meals were pretty good again, although bread remained in short supply."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 19 June 1942",
    "Another hot day passed with the usual routine aboard ship. The meals could have been better."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 20 June 1942",
    "Bill took part in gun drill before the Commanding Officer carried out his rounds. He was thoroughly chocka with the ship, and had another disparaging comment to make about the old man. Food remained rationed."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 21 June 1942",
    "The usual Sunday divisions and church were held. At 5.30 p.m. they passed Hawaii, with their arrival due the following day. The weather was good and the night was clear."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 22 June 1942",
    "The rest of the convoy entered Pearl Harbor first. The weather was a little rough and the wind was blowing hard as Tui made her way in. At the coal jetty she struck the jetty and dented her bows.\n\nLeave was restricted to the canteen, but Bill was able to go around the harbour in a launch. He saw for himself the damage left by the Japanese attack, including the wrecks of Arizona and Utah, and the badly damaged West Virginia. Later he gave some of the American boys rum, made friends and had a good time."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 23 June 1942",
    "Bill cut his hand on some wire during the day. Between watches he managed to get ashore, where he bought towels, photographic films, lotion and a few other things.\n\nHe met several more of the local chaps and brought them aboard to show them around the ship. Later he went to the movies and had a few wets before returning aboard at 11 p.m. for the middle watch."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 24 June 1942",
    "Bill went ashore at noon and made his way into Honolulu, where he had a swell time and bought some souvenirs. He met a Hawaiian girl who took a liking to him, and had photographs taken for nothing. Before they parted, they made a date for Friday, and Bill was determined to turn up.\n\nHe also saw Bert ashore, thoroughly hooped."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 25 June 1942",
    "There was a slight shower during the day, and Bill remained aboard on duty. He had the opportunity to look over the American aircraft carrier USS Enterprise, before strolling around to the coalyard."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 26 June 1942",
    "Bill went ashore at noon and travelled into Honolulu to see Ruth, keeping the date they had arranged two days earlier. His leave lasted until 5.30 p.m. They had one or two drinks, and Bill bought some more souvenirs.\n\nHe later went around to a place he calls “San Pon.”"
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 27 June 1942",
    "Bill was on duty and showed some American sailors around the ship. He also took the dog out for a run.\n\nLater, a party of American naval officers came aboard, and drinks were shared in the wardroom."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 28 June 1942",
    "After divisions, the old man went ashore. Bill was told that they should be home by the end of July.\n\nHe met Ruth again and went home with her, where they had a swell time. Bill stayed over his leave by twelve hours, but managed to get back aboard without trouble. Ruth, he thought, certainly had it bad for him."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 29 June 1942",
    "Bill was on duty as Quartermaster and remained around the dockyard, where he bought another couple of towels. The weather was very warm.\n\nThe ship was shifted during the day after Killegray blew a boiler."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 30 June 1942",
    "Bill had a haircut and a few wets before calling on Ruth and buying another couple of souvenirs. He had a bottle of rum ashore and was back aboard by 6 p.m.\n\nLater he went ashore again within the dockyard and had supper aboard San-Pan."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 1 July 1942",
    "Bill was on duty as Quartermaster. He went aboard a sampan, where he had supper and talked for a while."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 2 July 1942",
    "Bill visited the sick bay and had his eye seen to, as well as his best DR. He went ashore at 12.15 p.m., had a couple of drinks, including a “Zombie,” and had photographs taken with a Marine. He bought stockings, a shirt and other items, while Bert wore his blue suit.\n\nBill was back aboard by 6 p.m. and was now looking forward to leaving Pearl Harbor. There were more buzzes about their departure, but nothing definite yet."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 3 July 1942",
    "They went to sea at 7.30 a.m., taking a party of Americans with them to demonstrate how the gear aboard was worked. During the day they carried out a submarine attack and gunnery drill, returning to Pearl Harbor at 5 p.m.\n\nBill spent some time reading and cutting hair before turning in at 10 p.m."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 4 July 1942",
    "Bill had only one dollar left to go ashore with. He visited Waikiki, where he had a good time surfing, watched the dancing, and had a milkshake and hamburger. He had forgotten his pay book and had difficulty getting back aboard, eventually returning an hour and a half adrift."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 5 July 1942",
    "After divisions and church, the old man was not in too good a mood. It was certainly wet, and Bill was increasingly weary of the routine of divisions and rig of the day.\n\nThere was no leave for the men, although that suited Bill well enough, as he had said goodbye to his friends the previous night."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 6 July 1942",
    "They left Pearl Harbor at 3.15 p.m. and ran into a rough sea and high winds. Some of the boys were not too good in the heavy conditions.\n\nSupper was corned dog. Bill was thoroughly tired of the repetitive food: they had endured bacon and egg for fourteen days, then egg and sausages for fourteen days, and now it was egg and canned bacon.\n\nThe other ships could not make much speed, adding to his frustration with the voyage. He was fed up with the ship.\n\nAt 2 a.m., there was a great moonrise and many stars."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 10 July 1942",
    "Their speed was down to five knots, with the coal proving no good. Bill saw aircraft overhead, followed by an American aircraft carrier and four cruisers, which had them guessing for a while. There was word of a submarine somewhere in these waters.\n\nThe food was no good, with the same meals being served as before. Some of the boys were not feeling too good, and it was hot as the devil."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 11 July 1942",
    "They left the slower coal-burning ships behind and continued towards Palmyra Island to meet another ship. Bill was pleased to be steaming faster now that they were on their own.\n\nIt was a hot day, reaching 95°F (35°C), with a few showers passing through. Some of the boys wanted Bill to cut their hair, while his work aboard included washing paintwork and greasing the shrouds."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 12 July 1942",
    "Sunday began with action stations before the guns were cleaned. Food was becoming a problem, and the men were now rationed on bread.\n\nSome of the meat had gone rotten, leaving Bill to go without meals. He was plainly unimpressed with the food situation.\n\nThere was another unpleasant development aboard as well: crabs had broken out among the men. Bill could only hope that he managed to avoid catching them."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 13 July 1942",
    "They arrived off Fanning Island at 7 a.m. and waited outside while the British Administrator went aboard the merchant ship they were escorting. The old man was in a bad mood, and the Administrator did not come aboard their ship.\n\nThey sailed again at 9.30 a.m., only to turn back after discovering that they had forgotten to leave the mail. Bill spoke with a man from the island and learned that Americans had relieved the New Zealanders stationed there.\n\nThere was no more bread aboard. Bill was thoroughly fed up with the ship and would be glad when he was finally off it."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 14 July 1942",
    "Bill spent part of the day painting the mast, writing letters and reading. At 9.30 p.m. they crossed the Equator and, in Bill's eyes, were back in home waters."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 15 July 1942",
    "They went to action stations during the day. There was one or two tiffs among the men, and Bill was sure there would be fights before long. He would be glad when they reached Suva.\n\nThe weather was good and whales were sighted during the passage. Bill spent part of the day painting the topmast."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 16 July 1942",
    "The usual routine continued as they headed south, with Bill spending part of the day painting the ship. The galley fire was not working properly, and eggs were served yet again. Bill considered the food only moderate."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 17 July 1942",
    "It was hot as Hades during the day, with the usual routine continuing aboard. Heavy rain fell during the night, when it became a little cooler."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 18 July 1942",
    "The weather remained hot and showery. Bill cleaned the mess deck in preparation for rounds, while there were arguments aboard. Whales and sharks were sighted during the day, and the Second Lieutenant made some scenes."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 19 July 1942",
    "The usual Sunday divisions and church were held aboard. It remained hot as the devil, with showers passing through during the day."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 20 July 1942",
    "It was cooler today, with rather rough seas. More water was coming over the bows and gunwale as they continued south, but they would soon be in port. Bill also spent some time cutting hair."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 21 July 1942",
    "A wave came over the after deck and left Bill wet through as the ship continued through pretty rough seas. The Fiji Islands were sighted at 1 a.m., and conditions became a little calmer.\n\nAt 9 p.m. they crossed the International Date Line."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 23 July 1942",
    "Having crossed the International Date Line, they had jumped a day. Land was now in sight as they passed through Nanuku Passage, with Suva due the following morning."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 24 July 1942",
    "They were in port by 8 a.m. and fooled around aboard during the day. Bill went ashore at 4.30 p.m., but found there was nothing doing and considered Suva the worst place of all.\n\nLater he went dancing in the jungle, where he had some fun and tried kava. Leave expired at midnight, when he walked back aboard."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 25 July 1942",
    "Bill spent part of the day painting the ship. It rained, and as he was on duty he remained aboard, taking a siesta during the day. He spoke with some of the lads from Matai and Viti, and packed his gear and souvenirs in preparation for the journey home."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 26 July 1942",
    "Bill went ashore at 11 a.m., but found Suva a dismal place with nothing much to see. By chance he ran into an old shipmate from Matai. He was also lucky enough to accidentally run into Den and his friend, and they were pleased to see each other.\n\nThey had a few wets aboard, including rum. Bill gave Den a bottle of rum, showed him his souvenirs and gave him some of them, while they talked about their experiences."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 27 July 1942",
    "Bill was on duty when Den came aboard and stayed for supper. A couple of his old shipmates from Matai and Viti also came aboard during the day.\n\nBill came off duty at 9.30 p.m. He weighed Den down with books and other things before they parted, wishing him the best of luck and a quick return to New Zealand."
  ],
  [
    "Tuesday, 28 July 1942",
    "They said farewell to Suva at 6.30 a.m., with Bill hoping never to see the place again. The weather was calm, although they expected rougher seas as they headed towards New Zealand.\n\nThe boys spent time sorting out their gear for the approaching arrival home. The coal-burning ships accompanying them were smoking heavily as they steamed south."
  ],
  [
    "Wednesday, 29 July 1942",
    "It was misty and warm, with fairly rough seas as they continued towards New Zealand. The night brought brilliant moonlight. The food aboard was still no good."
  ],
  [
    "Thursday, 30 July 1942",
    "They went to action stations during the day. The weather was darn cold as they drew nearer to New Zealand, and rain fell heavily. Bill also scalded his eyes."
  ],
  [
    "Friday, 31 July 1942",
    "It was colder as they continued south. Sanda was nearly out of coal, with only 25 tons remaining, and their speed had been reduced to seven knots. Bill was frustrated by the delay, expecting that they would now be a day later reaching New Zealand. He had lost interest in the voyage."
  ],
  [
    "Saturday, 1 August 1942",
    "It was a pretty good day, but the difficulties among the coal-burning ships continued. Tui took Scarba in tow, while Inchkeith towed Sanda, reducing their progress to only three knots.\n\nBill hoped they would not encounter a gale. At such a slow speed, he wondered when they would finally reach port."
  ],
  [
    "Sunday, 2 August 1942",
    "Sunday divisions and church were held aboard. The weather was good, but they were still making only three and a half knots. At that rate, Bill expected them to reach port about Tuesday."
  ],
  [
    "Monday, 3 August 1942",
    "The weather was good, and at 6 a.m. they sighted Cape Brett. Muritai and James Cosgrove came out to meet them, with Muritai taking Sanda in tow. Bill saw Bud aboard Muritai.\n\nWith home now close, Bill packed his souvenirs. At 7.30 p.m. they passed Whangarei Heads as they continued south towards Auckland."
  ]
